const ANSWER_KEY = {"1": "A", "2": "C", "3": "A", "4": "D", "5": "D", "6": "A", "7": "D", "8": "B", "9": "D", "10": "E", "11": "C", "12": "D", "13": "D", "14": "D", "15": "A", "16": "C", "17": "E", "18": "E", "19": "C", "20": "D", "21": "D", "22": "E", "23": "A", "24": "A", "25": "C", "26": "E", "27": "B", "28": "B", "29": "D", "30": "B", "31": "D", "32": "E", "33": "B", "34": "D", "35": "B", "36": "A", "37": "D", "38": "F", "39": "D", "40": "A", "41": "B", "42": "E", "43": "E", "44": "G", "45": "B", "46": "D", "47": "C", "48": "B", "49": "A", "50": "A"};
const EXPLANATIONS = {"1": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Pyelonephritis typically presents with fever, nausea, vomiting, and flank pain with associated costovertebral angle tenderness on physical examination. It commonly occurs from ascending lower urinary tract infections. Diagnosis of pyelonephritis is made based on clinical history, physical examination, and laboratory studies, with urinalysis and urine culture playing a critical role. Urinalysis in pyelonephritis often shows greater than 10 WBC/hpf. Pyelonephritis increases the risk for obstetric complications such as preterm labor. In pregnancy, pyelonephritis is treated with intravenous antibiotics.</p><p>Based on this patient's presentation, which suggests pyelonephritis, the most appropriate next step is a urine culture. Pyelonephritis, an infection of the upper urinary tract and kidneys, is typically caused by the retrograde spread of bacterial infection from the lower urinary tract. It is characterized by fever, leukocytosis (greater than 10 WBC/hpf), nausea, vomiting, and flank pain with associated costovertebral angle tenderness. Symptoms of cystitis, such as dysuria, urinary urgency, and urinary frequency, are typically present. In pregnancy, asymptomatic bacteriuria increases the risk for pyelonephritis. Pyelonephritis increases the risk for obstetric complications such as preterm labor, which is defined as contractions with cervical change occurring after 20 and before 37 weeks of gestation. The risk to fetal survival is greater the earlier preterm labor occurs. Diagnosis of pyelonephritis is made based on clinical history, physical examination, and laboratory studies, with urinalysis and urine culture playing a critical role. In cases of diagnostic uncertainty or where competing differential diagnoses may be present, imaging may be required to rule out appendicitis or nephrolithiasis if such conditions cannot be clinically excluded. Treatment of pyelonephritis in a pregnant patient, even if obstetric complications have not occurred, as in this case, generally includes hospital admission for intravenous antibiotics. Even after the initial treatment course, suppressive antibiotics may be continued throughout the remainder of the pregnancy to prevent recurrent pyelonephritis.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Barium enema (Choice B) is appropriate in the diagnostic evaluation of volvulus or other structural lower gastrointestinal pathology. It does not offer specific diagnostic information in the evaluation of pyelonephritis or genitourinary pathology.</p><p>CT scan of the abdomen (Choice C) is useful in the evaluation of many intra-abdominal conditions, including but not limited to kidney stones, renal abscesses, retroperitoneal bleeding, malignancies, bowel obstructions, peritonitis, and diverticulitis. In cases where the diagnosis is uncertain, CT scan of the abdomen can be potentially appropriate, even in pregnancy. Where reasonable, measures to decrease fetal radiation exposure should be used; however, a critical test should not be avoided purely because of concern over fetal radiation risk. This patient's urinalysis does not suggest a kidney stone (limited RBC/hpf) and does suggest a urinary tract infection (pyuria), making a urine culture the most important next step in diagnosis.</p><p>Intravenous pyelography (IVP) (Choice D) is used to image the renal collecting system and bladder. IVP can be used in the evaluation of hematuria and kidney stones, though its use is less common given advances in CT scan and MRI technologies.</p><p>Laparoscopy (Choice E) is indicated in the management of ruptured ectopic pregnancy or in the diagnosis of endometriosis, among other conditions. This patient shows clinical evidence of a urinary tract infection without surgical complication, so invasive procedures would be unnecessary.</p>", "2": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Cyclophosphamide causes acute hemorrhagic cystitis, which presents with urinary urgency and frequency along with gross hematuria. Urinalysis shows increased red blood cells and an absence of bacteria, and urine culture generally shows no growth.</p><p>Cystitis, inflammation of the bladder, can occur from infectious and noninfectious agents. It frequently arises as a result of infection, especially from bacteria entering the urethra and ascending to the bladder. Symptoms include dysuria, frequency, urgency, and suprapubic discomfort in such cases. There are multiple causes of cystitis that are noninfectious, including the chemotherapeutic agent cyclophosphamide. Cyclophosphamide is known to cause acute hemorrhagic cystitis, which presents with urinary urgency and frequency along with gross hematuria. Urinalysis will show increased red blood cells per high power field and an absence of bacteria, and urine culture will generally show no growth. This patient's presentation and use of cyclophosphamide make cystitis the most likely diagnosis.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</b></p><p>Acute urethral syndrome (Choice A), otherwise known as urethral pain syndrome, describes dysuria without any identifiable infectious or inflammatory cause of urethral pain. Urinalysis in such cases is within normal limits. The presence of hematuria in this patient excludes this diagnosis.</p><p>Bladder calculi (Choice B) may be asymptomatic if small and nonobstructive. In cases where a bladder calculus is large, it may cause bleeding or intermittent pain if it obstructs urinary flow. Pain and tenderness may be intermittent, and cyclophosphamide is not a risk factor for bladder calculi, whereas it is a risk factor for hemorrhagic cystitis.</p><p>Diabetes insipidus (Choice D) describes either central or nephrogenic failure of the kidney to reclaim free water. Patients present with copious amounts of dilute urine, and hematuria is not a feature.</p><p>Lithium intoxication (Choice E) can present with many symptoms and signs depending on the amount ingested, time since ingestion, and any coingestions. Symptoms include nausea, vomiting, tremors, rigidity, clonus, a decreased level of consciousness, coma, seizures, autonomic instability, and/or serotonin syndrome. Hemorrhagic cystitis is not a recognized feature.</p>", "3": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Detrusor instability causes urge incontinence, which presents with a sudden urge to urinate followed by the leakage of a variable amount of urine, in the absence of a cough, sneeze, or Valsalva. Treatment includes strengthening the pelvic floor, bladder training or timed voiding, and antimuscarinic agents.</p><p>Detrusor instability results in overactive bladder and urge incontinence. As seen in this patient, urge incontinence typically presents with the urge to urinate followed by the leakage of a variable amount of urine. Urge incontinence is most commonly associated with neurologic disorders (eg, multiple sclerosis, spinal stenosis, or spinal cord injuries) but may also be idiopathic. Physical examination and laboratory studies will be unremarkable. Postvoid urinary bladder residual volume will generally be normal. When evaluating urinary incontinence, however, it is important to rule out an associated urinary tract infection, especially when the urinary incontinence is new in onset. Treatment of urge incontinence includes strengthening the pelvic floor, weight loss, minimizing caffeine and alcohol intake, bladder training or timed voiding, and antimuscarinic agents (eg, oxybutynin).</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Increased urethral mobility (Choice B) is commonly a cause of stress incontinence. Stress incontinence is triggered by an increase in intra-abdominal pressure, such as a coughing or sneezing event. This patient's sudden need to urinate in the absence of coughing or sneezing makes this unlikely.</p><p>Medication adverse effect (Choice C) is unlikely to be the cause of this patient's incontinence. This patient is being treated with a diuretic for her hypertension, which will cause increased urine output. However, diuretics are not associated with sudden-onset detrusor spasm (as seen in this patient) and make this cause less likely.</p><p>Pelvic relaxation (Choice D) is associated with stress incontinence. This is commonly seen in patients who have undergone multiple vaginal deliveries, those with obesity, and in men after prostate surgery. The sudden and episodic nature of her symptoms makes this unlikely.</p><p>Poor intrinsic sphincter function (Choice E) is seen with stress incontinence. When there is poor intrinsic sphincter function, any increase in intra-abdominal pressure can lead to expulsion of urine. However, this patient's symptoms are sudden in nature, in the absence of a cough, sneeze, or Valsalva, making this unlikely.</p>", "4": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Stress urinary incontinence presents with the leakage of urine with increased intra-abdominal pressure from physical activity or exertion, most commonly in multiparous patients due to weakness of the pelvic floor muscles and the subsequent loss of proximal support of the urethra. This pelvic floor weakness causes inferior displacement of the vesicourethral junction and hypermobility of the urethra with abdominal pressure. Treatment includes pelvic floor exercises, weight loss, pessary device placement, or surgical correction.</p><p>This patient presents with symptoms of stress urinary incontinence, most likely caused by urethral hypermobility. Stress urinary incontinence is common in multiparous patients and in patients following prostate surgery due to weakness of the muscles and connective tissues of the pelvic floor. In multiparous patients, this is typically secondary to pregnancy with resultant mechanical stress on the vaginal wall and pelvic floor. This weakness of the pelvic floor in turn compromises the support of pelvic structures, including the uterus, bladder, urethra, and rectum. Increased abdominal pressure from activity or exertion (eg, coughing, sneezing, laughing, running, heavy lifting) causes the vesicourethral junction to move out of its normal position, preventing sphincter closure, and resulting in the subsequent leakage of urine. Stress urinary incontinence is commonly associated with urethral hypermobility, as evidenced by deviation greater than 30 degrees from the horizontal of an inserted cotton-tipped applicator with straining. Stress incontinence can also be caused by a urethrocele or cystocele, although these would present with additional findings on pelvic examination. The treatment of stress incontinence includes pelvic floor exercises and weight loss, with the potential need for pessary device placement or surgical correction if there is persistent incontinence.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, E, and F.</b></p><p>Detrusor muscle hyperactivity (Choice A) causes urge incontinence, which presents with the urge to urinate followed by the leakage of a variable amount of urine, most commonly associated with neurologic disorders. Urethral hypermobility will not be present.</p><p>Detrusor muscle hypotonia (Choice B) is seen in patients with neurogenic bladder. While patients with neurogenic bladder can have episodic leaking of urine because of increased volumes, this would occur at random times as well as with coughing. Additionally, a history of spinal cord injury or other central nervous system pathology would be expected in this patient. A normal postvoid residual of less than 100 mL of urine would not be expected with neurogenic bladder.</p><p>Escherichia coli infection (Choice C) is a common cause of urinary tract infection (UTI). However, this is unlikely in this patient. Her symptoms have been increasing for the last year, and she reports no dysuria, which would be expected in a patient with a UTI.</p><p>Urethral obstruction (Choice E) is unlikely in this patient. A patient with urethral obstruction will present with inability to urinate, abdominal discomfort due to bladder distention, and potentially fevers and/or sepsis if pyelonephritis has developed. This patient is able to void completely, making obstruction unlikely.</p><p>Vesicovaginal fistula (Choice F) can be a consequence of gynecologic surgery, pelvic pathology, or radiation therapy. It typically presents with painless urinary leakage from the bladder into the vagina and a pool of urine present in the vaginal canal on pelvic examination. This patient does not have these symptoms.</p>", "5": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The ureter's close anatomic relationship to vessels, reproductive organs, and gastrointestinal organs puts it at risk for iatrogenic injury during pelvic surgery, most commonly during hysterectomy. Ureteral injury may present postoperatively with unilateral colicky flank pain, hematuria, fever, watery vaginal discharge, and increased serum creatinine concentrations. Imaging will show hydronephrosis and hydroureter. Risk factors for ureteral injury include extensive scarring and adhesions.</p><p>This patient's findings are most likely caused by surgical ligation of the ureter during hysterectomy. Hysterectomy is the most common cause of iatrogenic ureteral injuries, though ureteral injuries can occur as a complication from any pelvic surgery. The ureter's anatomic course explains this, as it travels deep to the ovarian vessels, superficial to the iliac vessels, deep to the uterine arteries, and then passes close to the cervix as it travels through the broad ligament of the uterus and then enters the bladder. The ureter is also closely associated with the gastrointestinal tract at multiple points along its path. Ureteral injuries can involve contusion, devascularization, ligation, transection, and electrothermal injury. Many centers routinely perform cystoscopy at the end of cases to assess the patency of the ureters. If the injury is found intraoperatively, urgent intraoperative consultation with a urologist is indicated. However, most ureteral injuries are not diagnosed until the postoperative period. Ureteral injury may present postoperatively with unilateral colicky flank pain, hematuria, fever, watery vaginal discharge, and increased serum creatinine concentrations. Imaging will show hydronephrosis and hydroureter. Risk factors for ureteral injury include extensive scarring and adhesions, as seen in this patient.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, E, and F.</b></p><p>Pelvic abscess (Choice A) can occur as a complication of hysterectomy, but it usually presents 5 to 10 days postoperatively with abdominal pain, fever, and ileus. Flank tenderness, hematuria, and hydronephrosis are not associated with this diagnosis.</p><p>Pyelonephritis (Choice B) can present with fever, nausea, vomiting, costovertebral angle tenderness, and pyuria. If obstruction and urinary stasis are the cause of the infection, hydronephrosis and hydroureter can be present. While this patient is at risk for pyelonephritis, its occurrence closely following pelvic surgery is suggestive of ureteral ligation as the underlying cause.</p><p>Renal calculus (Choice C) can present with colicky flank pain radiating to the groin, gross hematuria, difficulty voiding, and obstructive hydronephrosis. Fever is uncommon unless there is a superimposed infection such as pyelonephritis.</p><p>Ureteral obstruction secondary to endometriosis (Choice E) is rare; when it does occur, presentation is usually indolent with colicky flank pain, hydronephrosis, and hematuria. The initial presentation of this patient's ureteral obstruction is postoperative, not preoperative, suggestive of an iatrogenic injury.</p><p>Ureterovaginal fistula (Choice F) can occur as a delayed complication of pelvic surgery, pelvic radiation, or pelvic malignancy. While presentation can include constitutional symptoms such as fever and flank pain, this patient does not have abrupt-onset urinary incontinence or a high-volume, clear, watery vaginal discharge, as is seen in ureterovaginal fistulae.</p>", "6": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Diabetes during pregnancy increases the risk for congenital anomalies such as neural tube defects, cardiac malformations, and craniofacial defects due to the teratogenic effects of hyperglycemia. Maintaining blood glucose concentrations within target ranges, both before and throughout pregnancy, is crucial to minimize these adverse fetal outcomes.</p><p>Given this patient's history of type 1 diabetes mellitus and retinopathy, her fetus is most at risk for congenital cardiac anomalies. Diabetes during pregnancy, especially when blood glucose concentrations are not maintained within target ranges, significantly increases the risk for congenital anomalies in the developing fetus due to the teratogenic effects of hyperglycemia. Increased blood glucose concentrations can severely disrupt normal cellular and molecular processes during critical periods of fetal development. For instance, neural tube defects such as spina bifida and anencephaly are frequently observed, as hyperglycemia impairs the normal closure of the neural tube. Additionally, cardiac malformations, including transposition of the great arteries and ventricular septal defects, are common and result from the detrimental impact of high glucose concentrations on the developing heart by impairing the migration and proliferation of cardiac progenitor cells. Caudal regression syndrome, a severe condition where the lower spine fails to develop properly, is another consequence strongly associated with maternal diabetes. Renal anomalies, such as renal agenesis and dysplastic kidneys, as well as gastrointestinal issues like small left colon syndrome, can arise from disrupted organogenesis due to hyperglycemia. Maternal hyperglycemia is also a risk factor for craniofacial defects like cleft lip and palate. These congenital anomalies collectively underscore the critical importance of maintaining blood glucose concentrations within target ranges, both before and throughout pregnancy, to minimize the risk for adverse fetal outcomes.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Cystic hygroma (Choice B) is a congenital malformation characterized by fluid-filled sacs resulting from lymphatic system blockages, often associated with chromosomal abnormalities like Turner syndrome and Noonan syndrome. It is not associated with maternal diabetes.</p><p>Diaphragmatic hernia (Choice C) is a birth defect where abdominal organs herniate into the chest because of an incompletely formed diaphragm. Risk factors include some genetic mutations as well as maternal smoking and/or alcohol use. Maternal diabetes is not associated with development of congenital diaphragmatic hernia.</p><p>Potter syndrome (Choice D) is characterized by severe kidney abnormalities leading to decreased amniotic fluid (oligohydramnios) and underdeveloped lungs (pulmonary hypoplasia). Risk factors include genetic mutations, maternal oligohydramnios, and exposure to medications such as ACE inhibitors, angiotensin II receptor blockers, and NSAIDs.</p><p>Trisomy 18 syndrome (Choice E) is a genetic disorder caused by an extra chromosome 18, leading to severe developmental delays and organ defects. Advanced maternal age is a risk factor, while diabetes is not.</p>", "7": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Cystic fibrosis (CF) is associated with recurrent pulmonary infections caused by the impaired clearance of mucus, microbes, and debris from the airways. CF is inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern and requires two copies of the recessive allele to develop the disease state.</p><p>Cystic fibrosis (CF) is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by a defect in the CFTR gene, leading to a deficiency in a chloride channel that secretes chloride in the lungs and gastrointestinal tract and reabsorbs chloride in sweat glands. This abnormal chloride transport causes decreased chloride and water secretion and increased water reabsorption, leading to abnormally viscous mucus. The thick mucus in the gastrointestinal tract contributes to pancreatic insufficiency, steatorrhea, and malabsorption, leading to a deficiency of fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K. In the lungs, the disease manifests as recurrent pneumonia, especially with Staphylococcus aureus and Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Repeat antibiotic exposure increases the risk for colonization with resistant organisms. Obstruction and destruction of the airways leads to hypoxemia, air trapping, and bronchiectasis. Physical examination may disclose hypertrophic osteoarthropathy (clubbing), cyanosis, crackles and rhonchi, and hyperresonance to percussion of the chest. Imaging findings include hyperinflation, bronchiectasis with peribronchial cuffing, and diffuse nonspecific inflammatory changes. Given that this patient has a child with CF from a previous marriage but does not report a history of CF herself, the patient is a carrier of CF. As a result of this, the most appropriate screening would be to test her current partner for carrier state as well via paternal DNA/molecular testing, as CF is autosomal recessive and requires two copies of the recessive allele to develop the disease state.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, E, F, G, H, and I.</b></p><p>Amniocentesis (Choice A) is most commonly used in the evaluation of genetic abnormalities of the fetus. However, given the known carrier state of the patient, a less invasive/risky method of evaluation is preferable, such as parental DNA/molecular testing.</p><p>Chorionic villus sampling (Choice B) can be used to assess the karyotype of the fetus. This would not be appropriate as CF does not cause karyotypic abnormalities, and testing would be invasive and unnecessary.</p><p>Maternal DNA/molecular testing (Choice C) is not needed for this patient. Given that she has already given birth to a child with CF, but does not have CF herself, this confirms that she is a carrier of CF. Maternal testing would offer no new information in this case.</p><p>Maternal karyotype analysis (Choice E) and paternal karyotype analysis (Choice F) would not be helpful in this case. CF does not cause karyotypic changes, and thus testing would provide no new diagnostic information.</p><p>Maternal measurement of serum-specific immunoglobulin G (IgG) and M (IgM) concentrations (Choice G) would not be helpful in this case. Testing of maternal IgG or IgM concentrations is useful in the evaluation of congenital infections, maternal infections, or hepatitis. IgG and IgM play no role in the inheritance of CF.</p><p>Paternal measurement of serum-specific immunoglobulin G (IgG) and M (IgM) concentrations (Choice H) would not be helpful in this case. It can be used to identify a source of a congenital or maternal infection, though it is rarely performed. CF inheritance is not affected by paternal IgG or IgM concentrations.</p><p>Maternal serum triple screen aneuploidy calculation (Choice I) is not indicated in the assessment of CF risk. This screen checks α-fetoprotein, human chorionic gonadotropin, and unconjugated estriol. This information can help assess for the presence of certain chromosome abnormalities or neural tube defects.</p>", "8": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Hemolytic disease of the newborn is caused by maternal alloimmunization, typically involving Rh or ABO incompatibility, leading to neonatal anemia, jaundice, hepatosplenomegaly, and, in severe cases, hydrops fetalis. It can be prevented by administration of Rho(D) immune globulin to Rh-negative mothers at 28 weeks' gestation and within 72 hours postpartum.</p><p>Hemolytic disease of the newborn (HDN) is a condition characterized by the destruction of fetal red blood cells due to maternal alloimmunization, typically involving Rh or ABO blood group incompatibility. Historically, HDN was a major cause of neonatal morbidity and mortality before the advent of Rh immunoglobulin prophylaxis. Clinically, affected newborns may present with jaundice, anemia, hepatosplenomegaly, and hydrops fetalis in severe cases. Physical examination often discloses pallor, tachycardia, and in severe cases, generalized edema. Pathophysiologically, maternal IgG antibodies cross the placenta and target fetal erythrocytes for destruction, leading to hemolysis and subsequent bilirubin production. Preventative measures include antenatal administration of Rh immunoglobulin to Rh-negative mothers at 28 weeks' gestation and within 72 hours postpartum, as well as following any potential fetomaternal hemorrhage events. Close monitoring of at-risk pregnancies through serial ultrasonography and Doppler studies of the middle cerebral artery can also help in early detection and management.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, and D.</b></p><p>Administration of glucocorticoids after her first pregnancy (Choice A) would not prevent HDN in subsequent pregnancies because they do not address maternal alloimmunization. Prevention requires Rh immunoglobulin to prevent the mother's immune system from producing antibodies against fetal Rh antigens.</p><p>Administration of Rho(D) immune globulin early in her current pregnancy (Choice C) could prevent the development of HDN. However, administration of Rho(D) immune globulin after the first pregnancy would have prevented maternal sensitization and is more likely to prevent this condition overall.</p><p>Maternal plasmapheresis followed by administration of Rho(D) immune globulin early in her current pregnancy (Choice D) can be useful in the treatment of HDN, particularly in severe cases where conventional therapies are insufficient. It works by decreasing the concentrations of maternal antibodies that target fetal red blood cells, thereby decreasing the rate of hemolysis in the fetus. However, administration of Rho(D) immune globulin after the first pregnancy would have prevented maternal sensitization and is more likely to prevent this condition overall.</p>", "9": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Rh incompatibility testing occurs during the first trimester of pregnancy for Rh-negative patients and is repeated at around 28 weeks' gestation and again postpartum. Early identification and management of at-risk pregnancies are essential to mitigate the severe consequences associated with Rh incompatibility. Administration of Rho(D) immune globulin at approximately 28 weeks' gestation and within 72 hours postpartum can prevent alloimmunization. This condition can lead to hemolytic disease of the newborn, causing fetal anemia, hyperbilirubinemia, and potentially severe complications like hydrops fetalis.</p><p>Screening for Rh incompatibility is done during the first trimester and is repeated around 28 weeks' gestation and again postpartum, and involves performing an indirect Coombs test to detect anti-D antibodies in Rh-negative pregnant patients. Rh incompatibility arises when an Rh-negative mother is pregnant with an Rh-positive fetus, leading to maternal alloimmunization against fetal red blood cells. This immunologic response can result in hemolytic disease of the newborn, characterized by fetal anemia, hyperbilirubinemia, and in severe cases, hydrops fetalis. Pathophysiologically, maternal sensitization to the Rh antigen typically occurs during a patient's first pregnancy, often at the time of delivery when fetal red cells enter the maternal circulation. In subsequent pregnancies, maternal IgG antibodies directed against the Rh antigen can cross the placenta, leading to fetal red cell hemolysis. If the mother is Rh-negative and has not been sensitized, administration of Rho(D) immune globulin at approximately 28 weeks' gestation and within 72 hours postpartum can prevent alloimmunization. This prophylactic intervention inhibits maternal immune sensitization to the Rh antigen. Early identification and management of at-risk pregnancies are essential to mitigate the severe consequences associated with Rh incompatibility. Monitoring through serial ultrasonography and fetal blood sampling may be necessary to manage these pregnancies effectively.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Fetal nonstress test (Choice A) is used after 28 weeks of gestation to monitor fetal well-being in high-risk pregnancies, such as those with intrauterine growth restriction, preeclampsia, or diabetes. It assesses fetal heart rate accelerations in response to movements. Absence of accelerations may indicate the need for further evaluation. Given this patient's normal examination, this is not warranted at this time.</p><p>Quad screening (Choice B) is used between 15 and 20 weeks of gestation to assess the risk for certain fetal chromosomal abnormalities and neural tube defects. It measures concentrations of four substances in the mother's blood: α-fetoprotein, human chorionic gonadotropin, estriol, and inhibin-A. Rh retesting is more appropriate at this time.</p><p>Repeat fetal ultrasonography (Choice C) is unnecessary in this case. Given the reassuring physical examination and fetal heart tones today, and ultrasonography at 18 weeks that was grossly normal, it is unlikely that repeat ultrasonography would find any new abnormalities.</p><p>Testing for Group B streptococcal infection (Choice E) is typically performed between 35 and 37 weeks of gestation. In a Group B streptococcal-infected mother who gives birth, the fetus is at risk for sepsis, pneumonia, or meningitis, which can lead to severe complications or death if untreated.</p>", "10": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Prepregnancy visits allow a patient to optimize the chances of having a successful pregnancy with a healthy child. Important aspects of these visits include ascertaining immunity to varicella and rubella, encouraging abstinence from substance use, changing teratogenic medications, and prescribing folic acid therapy to prevent neural tube defects. Moderate physical activity has been shown to decrease the risks for complications such as gestational diabetes, obesity, macrosomia, lumbopelvic pain, and preeclampsia.</p><p>Prepregnancy visits allow a healthcare provider to optimize a patient's chances of having a successful pregnancy and healthy child. The purposes of these visits should be to identify risks to the mother or fetus and to intervene when possible to decrease these risks. Typical assessments include evaluating medical conditions (eg, obesity, diabetes mellitus, hypertension), social behaviors (eg, substance use), exposure to teratogens (eg, medications), genetic conditions or family history, and vaccination records. Laboratory studies commonly include rubella and varicella serology to ensure immunity, as well as sexually transmitted infection screening in patients at high risk (eg, multiple sexual partners, no barrier protection). Finally, interventions include folic acid supplementation, abstinence from substance use, medication changes as needed, optimization of medical conditions, and required vaccinations. This patient's level of physical activity has not been shown to increase the risk for adverse outcomes in healthy women with uncomplicated singleton pregnancies. Regular exercise has been shown to decrease the risk for multiple pregnancy complications such as gestational diabetes, obesity, macrosomia, lumbopelvic pain, and preeclampsia. The patient should be counseled to exercise caution with activities that carry risk for impacts or falls.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Chorioamnionitis (Choice A) is a bacterial infection of the fetal membranes and commonly occurs in the setting of prelabor or prolonged rupture of membranes. Maternal fever and leukocytosis are characteristic, and the uterus may be tender to palpation. Maternal exercise has not been shown to increase the risk for chorioamnionitis.</p><p>Oligohydramnios (Choice B) describes an inadequate amount of amniotic fluid that can cause growth restriction and structural abnormalities of the fetus. Causes include prelabor rupture of the membranes, uteroplacental insufficiency, and congenital malformations of the fetal genitourinary tract (eg, posterior urethral valves or renal agenesis). It is not associated with maternal physical activity.</p><p>Placenta previa (Choice C) is an abnormal positioning of placental tissue such that the placenta partially or entirely overlies the internal cervical os. Placenta previa is usually detected on routine prenatal ultrasonography. Its most common presenting symptom is third trimester painless vaginal bleeding. Maternal exercise is not associated with an increased risk for placenta previa.</p><p>Postdates pregnancy (Choice D) is defined as a gestation that persists to or beyond 42 weeks and is associated with maternal and fetal complications. Risk factors include primiparity, history of prior postdates pregnancy, and obesity. Maternal exercise is not associated with postdates pregnancy and decreases the risk for obesity.</p>", "11": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Cervical insufficiency exists when the cervix is unable to retain a pregnancy in the absence of the signs and symptoms of labor during the second or early third trimester. Risk factors include a history of cervical insufficiency with past pregnancies and prior cervical procedures. Cervical insufficiency can be treated with cervical cerclage.</p><p>The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists defines cervical insufficiency as the inability of the cervix to contain the pregnancy (ie, the fetus, placenta, and amniotic sac) without the presence of contractions, labor, or both, typically during the second or early third trimester. The condition is associated with miscarriage, preterm or premature rupture of the membranes, and preterm birth. Classically, when symptomatic, it presents with feelings of fullness in the lower pelvis in the absence of contractions or labor. Speculum examination may show a dilated or effaced cervix, or protrusion of the pregnancy through the cervical os into the vagina. Mild cases without dilation of the external os, as in this patient, may go undetected. Cervical length measurements may show a shortened cervix with a cervical length less than 15 to 25 mm. Cervical funneling, in which the internal os is dilated and the functional cervical length is shortened, can also be seen. Treatment is indicated if the degree of cervical insufficiency is significant enough to threaten the pregnancy. This is accomplished through cervical cerclage, which is the placement of a suture into the cervical os to narrow the canal and maintain a closed cervix. The suture is then removed at the time of labor or around 36 to 38 weeks. Prior procedures on the cervix, such as loop electrosurgical excision or conization, are risk factors for cervical insufficiency.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</b></p><p>Abruptio placentae (Choice A) presents with vaginal bleeding, severe uterine pain, and tetanic contractions, typically in the third trimester. It commonly results from lower abdominal trauma, smoking, or cocaine use.</p><p>Cervical cancer (Choice B) is classically associated with infection from human papillomavirus high-risk strains 16, 18, 31, and 33. It presents indolently until advanced, locally invasive, or metastatic; vaginal bleeding or postcoital spotting may be the presenting symptom. Smoking, immunosuppression, and multiple sexual partners are risk factors.</p><p>Incomplete abortion (Choice D) occurs when a fetus expires and there is partial expulsion of fetal tissue through an open cervical os. It is typically associated with pelvic pain and vaginal bleeding. Treatment consists of expectant management, uterine aspiration, or dilation and curettage. This patient's external cervical os is closed and her pelvic ultrasonography findings are more consistent with cervical insufficiency.</p><p>Subchorionic hemorrhage (Choice E) is a common sonographic abnormality and is the most common cause of vaginal bleeding in the first half of pregnancy. It occurs due to partial detachment of the chorionic membrane from the endometrium. Large hematomas are associated with an increased risk for spontaneous abortion, abruptio placentae, and preterm labor.</p>", "12": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Physiologic anemia during pregnancy results from increased plasma volume causing hemodilution, typically decreasing hemoglobin concentrations. Iron deficiency anemia is common as a result of increased iron demands for fetal and placental development. Symptoms include fatigue and pallor. Management involves dietary changes, supplements, and routine screening.</p><p>Physiologic anemia during pregnancy is a common condition resulting from the increased plasma volume that expands more significantly than the red cell mass, leading to hemodilution. This dilutional effect typically manifests in the second trimester and peaks in the third trimester, causing a decrease in hemoglobin concentration. The normal hemoglobin concentration in nonpregnant patients ranges from 12 to 16 g/dL, but during pregnancy, it often drops to 10.5 to 11 g/dL. Iron deficiency anemia is a prevalent concern during pregnancy as a result of the increased iron demands for fetal development and placental growth, as well as the expansion of maternal red cell mass. The placenta requires significant amounts of iron to support its rapid growth and function, facilitating nutrient and oxygen transfer to the developing fetus. Additionally, the fetus relies on maternal iron stores for hematopoiesis and overall development, particularly in the second and third trimesters when fetal growth accelerates. Symptoms of iron deficiency anemia in pregnancy can include fatigue, pallor, and shortness of breath, which, if left untreated, may lead to adverse outcomes, such as preterm delivery and low birth weight. Routine screening for anemia, including a complete blood count and serum ferritin concentrations, is recommended during prenatal visits. Management typically involves dietary counseling to increase iron intake and supplementation with oral iron preparations, which may be necessary to meet the increased demands and prevent iron deficiency anemia. In some cases, intravenous iron therapy may be indicated for those who cannot tolerate oral supplements or have significant anemia.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Blood smear (Choice A) is indicated in a pregnant patient with unexplained microcytic or macrocytic anemia, abnormal red cell morphology, or signs of hemolysis. It aids in diagnosing conditions such as thalassemia, sickle cell disease, or hemolytic anemias. However, given this patient is healthy overall and has no overt signs of hematologic pathology, this would not be appropriate.</p><p>Hemoglobin electrophoresis (Choice B) would not be appropriate for this patient. While hemoglobin electrophoresis is indicated in a pregnant patient with unexplained microcytic anemia, this patient does not show any other laboratory results that would be suggestive of a thalassemia, sickle cell, or other hemoglobin variants. It is more likely this patient's diagnosis is physiologic anemia, and electrophoresis is not necessary.</p><p>Reticulocyte count (Choice C) assessment would not be beneficial in this patient. A reticulocyte count is useful in evaluating bone marrow function and responsiveness. Given this patient's pregnancy status, microcytosis, and mild anemia, it is more likely she has physiologic anemia and not bone marrow deficiency.</p><p>Test of the stool for occult blood (Choice E) would not be appropriate. Insidious loss of blood through slow gastrointestinal bleeds can be a leading cause of iron deficiency anemia but is commonly seen in an older population. This is unlikely to be the cause of this patient's anemia.</p>", "13": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Common causes of increased α-fetoprotein concentrations in maternal serum are an inaccurate gestational date, multiple gestations, and abdominal wall or neural tube defects. Omphaloceles, unlike gastroschisis, are commonly associated with other congenital defects, including aneuploidy, so genetic evaluation of the fetus beginning with a fetal karyotype is indicated.</p><p>The most common causes of increased α-fetoprotein (AFP) concentration in the maternal serum include an inaccurate gestational date, multiple gestations, abdominal wall defects (eg, gastroschisis, omphalocele), and neural tube defects (eg, anencephaly, spina bifida). Omphalocele refers to a defect in the abdominal wall of the fetus that allows herniation of the bowels and, occasionally, other abdominal organs, such as the liver. The defect occurs at the base of the umbilical cord, and the herniated contents are covered by a membranous sac. In contrast to gastroschisis, an abdominal wall malformation that allows evisceration of the bowels without a peritoneal membrane covering, omphaloceles are more commonly associated with other congenital defects. These include cardiovascular disease, gastrointestinal anomalies, aneuploidy, and genetic disorders, such as Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome. Genetic workup of the fetus beginning with a fetal karyotype is indicated. Amniocentesis is used to obtain fetal genetic material in patients at 15 to 20 weeks' gestation. Alternative methods include chorionic villus sampling and obtaining cell-free fetal DNA fragments from the mother's serum.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Amniotic fluid acetylcholinesterase activity (Choice A) indicates the presence of a neural tube defect and can be pursued in patients with increased maternal serum AFP and no definitive abnormalities on fetal ultrasonography. This fetus's omphalocele is the likely cause of the mother's increased AFP.</p><p>Amniotic fluid α-fetoprotein concentration (Choice B) is unlikely to provide any meaningful information for this patient, as maternal AFP is known to be significantly increased, and a cause has been found on ultrasonography. Further evaluation should focus on genetic analysis and searching for associated additional anomalies.</p><p>Fetal HLA type (Choice C) is not known to correlate with abdominal wall defects. A genetic workup for this fetus with an omphalocele is more appropriate.</p><p>Maternal serum β-hCG and estriol concentrations (Choice E) is incorrect. Serial measurement of serum β-hCG concentrations is often used in the evaluation of ectopic pregnancy or in screening for the development of gestational trophoblastic disease after a molar pregnancy. Serum estradiol concentrations are not routinely measured in maternal-fetal diagnostic workups. Genetic evaluation is more appropriate in this patient.</p>", "14": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Missed abortion, a subtype of spontaneous abortion, refers to asymptomatic fetal demise without the passage of products. Missed abortion can be diagnosed on ultrasonography with fetal demise and the presence of intrauterine gestational products and can be managed expectantly or with procedural intervention to evacuate the uterus.</p><p>Spontaneous abortion describes the noninduced loss of a fetus prior to 20 weeks' gestation, and is subdivided into threatened, inevitable, incomplete, complete, missed, and septic abortion. Threatened abortion presents with vaginal bleeding without an open os or the loss of products of conception. Inevitable abortion presents with vaginal bleeding and an open os and is referred to as an incomplete abortion once the partial passage of products begins. Complete abortion refers to the passage of all products and a closed os. Missed abortion refers to asymptomatic fetal demise without the passage of products. In septic abortion, infection occurs within the uterine cavity. This patient most likely experienced a missed abortion. Missed abortion can be diagnosed on ultrasonography with fetal demise and the presence of intrauterine gestational products and can be managed expectantly or with procedural intervention to evacuate the uterus.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Endometrial carcinoma (Choice A) often presents as abnormal uterine bleeding and is diagnosed by endometrial biopsy. It typically presents in patients who are age 45 years or older and often postmenopausal. A confirmed pregnancy on ultrasonography co-presenting with endometrial carcinoma would be rare.</p><p>Endometrial polyp (Choice B) is a cause of abnormal uterine bleeding as a result of overgrowth of tissue within the endometrial cavity. Polyps may be visible on ultrasonography if sufficiently large; otherwise, ultrasonography may be nondiagnostic. Direct visualization on hysteroscopy or hysterosalpingography is more likely to detect the presence of a polyp.</p><p>Hydatidiform mole (Choice C) may present with persistent nausea and vomiting, first trimester vaginal bleeding, early preeclampsia, and/or uterine size out of proportion to expected gestational age because of an abnormal fertilization of an ovum. Pelvic ultrasonography will show diffuse echogenic structures in the uterus and bilateral ovarian theca-lutein cysts.</p><p>Submucous leiomyoma uteri (Choice E) are benign neoplasms of the uterine myometrium. They are a potential cause of abnormal uterine bleeding and pelvic pain and are typically visible on ultrasonography. There would not be an abnormality within the endometrial cavity in a submucosal leiomyoma.</p>", "15": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: A heterotopic pregnancy is a pregnancy in which there exists dual gestation of both an ectopic and intrauterine pregnancy. It is a known complication of multiple embryo transfers and is a specific risk for assistive reproductive therapies.</p><p>Ectopic pregnancy is an abnormal pregnancy in which the fertilized ovum implants outside of the uterus, most commonly in a fallopian tube, but also on the ovary, within the peritoneal cavity, or in any nonendometrial location. It often presents with abdominal pain and vaginal bleeding in a patient with a missed or delayed period and positive pregnancy test. Ultrasonography can be used to further delineate between an intrauterine and ectopic pregnancy and may visualize an ectopic pregnancy depending on its size. Risk factors for ectopic pregnancy include but are not limited to a history of pelvic inflammatory disease, the presence of an IUD, use of assistive reproductive technology or in vitro fertilization/embryo transfer, history of endometriosis, and history of prior ectopic pregnancy. In cases of multiple ovulations or multiple embryo transfers, a heterotopic pregnancy can occur. A heterotopic pregnancy is a pregnancy in which there exists dual gestation of both an intrauterine and ectopic pregnancy. Complications of ectopic pregnancy include rupture, bleeding, and hemoperitoneum.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Corpus luteum cyst (Choice B) develops from a mature follicle after ovulation during the menstrual cycle. In some instances, the corpus luteum can form a cyst, which can potentially rupture. A ruptured corpus luteum cyst typically presents with the sudden onset of severe unilateral pelvic or lower abdominal pain. Pelvic ultrasonography will show a complex cystic mass or free fluid in the pelvis if ruptured. A corpus luteum cyst is located within, not adjacent to, an ovary.</p><p>Hydatidiform mole (Choice C) commonly presents with persistent nausea and vomiting, vaginal bleeding in the first trimester, early preeclampsia, and uterine size out of proportion to expected gestational age because of an abnormal fertilization of an ovum. Pelvic ultrasonography will show diffuse echogenic structures in the uterus and bilateral ovarian theca-lutein cysts.</p><p>Hydrosalpinx (Choice D) describes a fallopian tube filled with fluid, generally as a result of tubal occlusion or pelvic inflammatory disease. Ultrasonography would show an enlarged, dilated fallopian tube.</p><p>Ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome (Choice E) is an exaggerated follicular response to injected gonadotropins commonly used in infertility treatment. Symptoms include nausea, vomiting, and fluid retention, as well as shortness of breath and blood clots in severe cases. Ultrasonography shows bilaterally enlarged ovaries and may show adjacent pelvic fluid.</p>", "16": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Untreated preeclampsia can lead to eclampsia, defined by the occurrence of seizures. Eclampsia carries significant fetal and maternal morbidity and mortality. The initial treatment for eclampsia is the administration of magnesium sulfate and antihypertensive agents, including labetalol.</p><p>The cause of this patient's generalized tonic-clonic seizure is most likely eclampsia, a complication of untreated preeclampsia. Preeclampsia is caused by an abnormality of the placental spiral arteries and poor trophoblastic invasion, leading to placental hypoperfusion and the subsequent production of vasoactive hormones that cause generalized endothelial dysfunction, hypercoagulability, and vasospasm. It usually presents in the second or third trimester after 20 weeks' gestation with hypertension, proteinuria, and end-organ dysfunction (ie, headache, blurred vision, right upper quadrant pain), although it may also occur in the postpartum period. If untreated, preeclampsia can progress to eclampsia, characterized by maternal seizures, which can have profoundly adverse outcomes including maternal stroke, cardiac arrest, renal disease, or maternal or fetal death. Preeclampsia is treated with antihypertensive agents, eclampsia prophylaxis with magnesium sulfate, and delivery of the fetus and placenta. Magnesium sulfate is the initial treatment for eclampsia as well, as it is thought to increase the seizure threshold and has been shown to improve maternal mortality. Following administration of magnesium sulfate, the best next step is blood pressure control in a patient who has stopped seizing and has a nonimminent fetal presentation. Labetalol is a mixed α- and β-adrenergic blocking agent, used in the management of gestational hypertension and preeclampsia.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</b></p><p>Biophysical profile (Choice A), a type of noninvasive prenatal fetal testing, evaluates the developing pregnancy on many parameters, including amniotic fluid, movement, breathing, heart rate, and tone. It is a nonemergent test and does not offer meaningful diagnostic or therapeutic information in the setting of known eclampsia.</p><p>Intramuscular betamethasone (Choice B), a steroid, is used to promote fetal lung maturity in cases of preterm labor. While this medication may be needed, as this patient is at 34 weeks' gestation and the definitive treatment for eclampsia is delivery of the fetus, it does not address the immediate hypertensive emergency and resultant maternal neurologic dysfunction. Administration of this agent can occur after maternal resuscitation and stabilization.</p><p>Intravenous oxytocin (Choice D) causes uterine contraction and is frequently used to induce labor. It would be an appropriate choice to induce delivery after maternal resuscitation and stabilization, assuming an emergent cesarean delivery is not desired or performed.</p><p>Intravenous phenobarbital (Choice E), an antiepileptic medication, can be used to abort status epilepticus in cases where first- and second-line medications have been inadequate. This patient is no longer ictal and does not require emergent antiepileptics currently.</p>", "17": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Hydrops fetalis as a result of α-thalassemia major, or hemoglobin Barts hydrops fetalis, results from the deletion of all four alpha-globin genes, causing severe anemia, hypoxia, and generalized edema. This condition often leads to intrauterine fetal demise or stillbirth, with diagnosis confirmed through genetic testing and prenatal ultrasonography.</p><p>Hydrops fetalis as a result of α-thalassemia major, also known as homozygous α-thalassemia or hemoglobin Barts (Hb Barts) hydrops fetalis, is a severe genetic disorder resulting from the deletion of all four alpha-globin genes (αα/αα) on chromosome 16. This condition leads to a complete absence of alpha-globin chain production, causing the formation of abnormal Hb Barts (γ4), which has an extremely high oxygen affinity and is ineffective at oxygen delivery to tissues. The pathophysiology involves severe anemia, hypoxia, and compensatory extramedullary hematopoiesis, leading to massive hepatosplenomegaly and generalized edema. The lack of functional hemoglobin results in profound tissue hypoxia, hydrops fetalis, and often intrauterine fetal demise or stillbirth. Diagnosis is typically confirmed through genetic testing, showing the deletion of the alpha-globin genes, and prenatal ultrasonography may show signs of hydrops, such as skin edema, ascites, and pleural or pericardial effusion. Management of pregnancies with suspected α-thalassemia major involves close monitoring, and in some cases, intrauterine transfusions may be attempted to prolong fetal survival. Prognosis is generally poor, with most affected fetuses not surviving to term, and survivors requiring lifelong transfusions and iron chelation therapy. Genetic counseling is essential for parents.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Acute fetomaternal hemorrhage (Choice A) typically presents with sudden onset of decreased fetal movements, nonreassuring fetal heart rate patterns on monitoring, and may be accompanied by signs of fetal anemia, such as hydrops fetalis or fetal distress. While this is similar to what is seen in this patient, her two previous spontaneous abortions and history of α-thalassemia trait make this less likely.</p><p>Congenital sinus tachycardia (Choice B) is not likely the cause of the findings in this patient. Congenital sinus tachycardia would not account for the extreme generalized edema seen, nor the profound anemia.</p><p>Placental insufficiency (Choice C) typically presents with intrauterine growth restriction, decreased fetal movements, and may be detected through abnormal Doppler ultrasonography findings such as decreased blood flow in the umbilical artery. It would not explain the edema or the anemia.</p><p>Rh incompatibility (Choice D) can present with hemolytic disease of the newborn, characterized by jaundice, anemia, hepatosplenomegaly, and in severe cases, hydrops fetalis, which includes severe edema, ascites, and heart failure in the fetus. The fetus's Rh(D)-negative status makes this incorrect.</p>", "18": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Vasa previa, the condition in which blood vessels overlie the cervical os, presents with fetomaternal hemorrhage with rupture of membranes. Damage to the fetal blood vessels results in significant fetal hemorrhage and possible exsanguination and death. Management includes preventive elective cesarean delivery if the condition is known before delivery, or emergent cesarean delivery if found in real time.</p><p>Vasa previa is a condition in which the fetal blood vessels overlie the cervical os. It is commonly associated with placenta previa, the implantation of the placenta partially or completely over the internal cervical os. Given the position of the fetal blood vessels, they are at an increased risk for rupture with the onset of labor or instrumentation (amniotomy, in this case). The rupture of membranes can result in damage to the fetal blood vessels, which causes significant fetal hemorrhage and possible fetal exsanguination and death. It classically presents with bloody amniotic fluid immediately after rupture of membranes (spontaneous or after amniotomy), with rapid onset of severe fetal distress, marked by late decelerations and persistent bradycardia. A sinusoidal fetal heart rate pattern is an associated sign suggestive of fetomaternal hemorrhage. Risk factors for vasa previa include in vitro fertilization and multiple gestation pregnancies. Diagnosis is typically made prior to labor with routine screening ultrasonography, and management consists of elective cesarean delivery at 35 to 36 weeks' gestation. If the diagnosis is unknown and found at the time of labor, emergent cesarean delivery is indicated.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Cervical laceration (Choice A) presents with vaginal bleeding; however, in the case of pregnancy, a cervical laceration generally occurs after vaginal delivery. A cervical laceration results in maternal, not necessarily fetal, hemorrhage. It would not be expected to result in fetal bradycardia and distress.</p><p>Fetal scalp laceration (Choice B) is a potential complication of assisted delivery and may occur from forceps delivery, in the setting of placing a fetal scalp electrode, or during cesarean delivery. Bleeding is generally minimal unless coagulopathy is present and would not be expected to cause fetal bradycardia and hemodynamic instability.</p><p>Placenta accreta (Choice C) describes a placenta that has abnormally attached to the uterine myometrium. Normally, the placenta adheres to the endometrium. Risk factors include prior cesarean delivery or uterine surgery, increased maternal age, and multiparity. It is typically asymptomatic until delivery, when it causes postpartum hemorrhage because of retained products of conception.</p><p>Uterine rupture (Choice D) presents with acute abdominal pain and tenderness. It occurs when the muscular wall of the uterus tears. Patients are generally hemodynamically unstable and will show loss of fetal station. Risk factors include previous cesarean delivery and induction of labor, and treatment consists of emergent laparotomy.</p>", "19": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Fascial dehiscence is characterized by the separation of fascial layers beneath a surgical incision, often presenting with sudden serosanguineous drainage, bulging at the wound site, and lack of resistance to probing on examination. Dehiscence is most commonly observed within the first 2 weeks after surgery and can occur as a result of various factors such as infection, increased mechanical stress on the wound, or inadequate surgical technique. Treatment involves thorough wound cleaning, appropriate dressings, and possibly surgical intervention for reclosure.</p><p>Fascial dehiscence is characterized by the separation of fascial layers beneath a surgical incision, often presenting with sudden serosanguineous drainage, bulging at the wound site, and lack of resistance to probing on examination, as in this patient. Wound dehiscence may present with increased pain and visible separation of the wound edges, and on examination, there is often partial or complete reopening of the incision. Surgical wound dehiscence is a postoperative complication where a wound that was previously closed reopens along the surgical incision. Dehiscence can occur as a result of various factors such as infection, increased mechanical stress on the wound, or inadequate surgical technique, all of which compromise the integrity of the wound closure. Dehiscence is most commonly observed within the first 2 weeks after surgery, a critical period during which the wound is still gaining tensile strength. Dehiscence can lead to increased risk for infection and delayed healing and may result in evisceration of underlying tissues or organs. Prevention of surgical wound dehiscence includes meticulous surgical technique, proper wound care, and optimizing patient factors like nutrition and blood glucose management. Factors such as nutritional deficiencies, obesity, diabetes, and use of corticosteroids can predispose patients to wound dehiscence by impairing wound healing processes. Reducing mechanical stress on the wound and monitoring for infection also play crucial roles. Treatment involves thorough wound cleaning, appropriate dressings, and possibly surgical intervention for reclosure. Underlying factors, such as infection or nutritional deficiencies, should also be managed to promote healing.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</b></p><p>Enterocutaneous fistula (Choice A) is an abnormal connection that forms between the intestinal tract and the skin, often resulting in the drainage of gastrointestinal contents through the skin. This patient has serosanguineous drainage instead of drainage of gastrointestinal contents.</p><p>Subcutaneous hematoma (Choice B) is a localized collection of blood beneath the skin, often resulting from trauma, which causes discoloration, swelling, and tenderness in the affected area. A hematoma would be palpable on examination, and a probe would return blood.</p><p>Subfascial abscess (Choice D) is a purulent collection beneath the fascia, often causing pain, swelling, and systemic symptoms such as fever, chills, and malaise. Abscess would not explain the lack of resistance to the probed examination seen in this patient.</p><p>Wound infection (Choice E) commonly presents with fever, chills, erythema, and tenderness to the surgical site. Purulent drainage is often seen. This patient has minimal erythema and no purulent drainage or tenderness, making wound infection unlikely.</p>", "20": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Herpes simplex causes vesicular lesions on the vulva, vagina, perineum, or rectum, which progress to shallow, painful ulcers. Infection in a pregnant patient can spread to the infant during delivery via ascension through ruptured membranes or during fetal descent through the vaginal canal. Infection of the newborn can lead to vesicular skin lesions, ocular involvement, and/or encephalitis. In asymptomatic patients with no lesions at the time of delivery, the risk for transmission is very low and vaginal delivery is the most appropriate method of delivery.</p><p>Herpes simplex is characterized by painful vesicles in the genital area (eg, vulva, vagina, cervix, perineum) and may be associated with pain during urination. Most cases of genital herpes are caused by herpesvirus 2; however, herpesvirus 1 has also been associated with genital herpes. It typically begins with the formation of vesicles, which lyse and progress to shallow, painful ulcers with an erythematous border. This painful rash can be exacerbated during urination, in which acidic urine passes over open vesicles or ulcers. Other symptoms include fever, chills, and inguinal lymphadenopathy. In pregnant patients, the infection may pass to a newborn during delivery secondary to an ascending infection following spontaneous rupture of membranes or from passage through the infected lower genital tract. To prevent this intrapartum transmission of infection, acyclovir can be used to suppress outbreaks. A cesarean delivery is required if herpetic lesions or symptoms are present on the cervix, vagina, or vulva at the time of labor. Common complications in newborns include vesicular skin lesions and ocular involvement, while neonatal encephalitis is a potentially severe complication of infection. For this patient, the risk for transmission in the absence of lesions is very low, and vaginal delivery is the most appropriate method of delivery.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, and C.</b></p><p>Cesarean, because herpes simplex virus was active during this pregnancy (Choice A), is incorrect. The risk for transmission to a newborn in the absence of lesions is very low.</p><p>Cesarean, because she is not taking acyclovir prophylactically (Choice B), is incorrect. Prophylactic acyclovir can be used to suppress outbreaks as patients approach delivery but is not required in asymptomatic patients.</p><p>Vaginal, after a dose of intravenous acyclovir, because this will prevent neonatal infection (Choice C), is incorrect. A one-time dose of intravenous antivirals is not an effective treatment for an active herpes simplex outbreak. In patients with active lesions at the time of labor, cesarean delivery is most appropriate.</p>", "21": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Hypotension is a common adverse effect of epidural anesthesia as a result of sympathetic blockade and systemic vasodilation, and it can cause fetal hypoxia and bradycardia. It should be promptly addressed by decreasing the epidural anesthesia, infusing fluids, and/or administering vasopressors, and fetal bradycardia that persists despite treatment is an indication for emergent cesarean delivery.</p><p>The most likely cause of fetal bradycardia in this case is epidural-induced maternal hypotension. A healthy fetal heart rate is between 110 and 160 beats per minute (bpm) with 6 to 25 bpm of variability and spontaneous accelerations. Fetal bradycardia can be caused by congenital heart malformations, fetal hypoxia, or fetal distress. Fetal hypoxia can be caused by uteroplacental insufficiency, severe uterine contractions, and maternal hypotension or hypoxia. Hypotension is a common adverse effect of epidural anesthesia as a result of sympathetic blockade and systemic vasodilation. The patient's blood pressure should be promptly addressed by decreasing the epidural anesthesia, infusing fluids, and/or administering vasopressors. If fetal bradycardia persists despite treatment, emergent cesarean delivery is indicated.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Fetal atrioventricular block (Choice A) can affect children of mothers with systemic lupus erythematosus or Sjogren syndrome, as anti-Ro and anti-La antibodies can cross the placenta and interfere with normal formation of the fetal atrioventricular node and surrounding myocardium. In this patient with an uncomplicated pregnancy and an abrupt change in the fetal heart rate that corresponds with a change in the mother's hemodynamics, bradycardia caused by maternal hypotension is much more likely.</p><p>Fetal cord compression (Choice B) commonly occurs during active labor and presents with variable decelerations, defined as an abrupt decrease in heart rate of at least 15 bpm that lasts at least 15 seconds, but less than 2 minutes. Persistent bradycardia is not consistent with variable decelerations, and occurrence after placement of an epidural indicates that maternal hypotension is the likely cause.</p><p>Fetal head compression (Choice C) is also a common occurrence during labor when the fetal head becomes engaged in the maternal pelvis. It presents with early decelerations on the fetal heart rate tracing, defined as a gradual decrease in heart rate that mirrors the uterine contraction. This is considered a normal finding and requires no intervention.</p><p>Maternal vasovagal response (Choice E) can cause transient maternal hypotension and fetal bradycardia that recover spontaneously. A lack of spontaneous recovery and occurrence 15 minutes after placement of epidural anesthesia, which commonly causes hypotension through sympathetic blockade, make epidural-induced maternal hypotension the more likely cause.</p>", "22": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Group B streptococcus (GBS) is a common cause of infection in pregnant patients. As maternal colonization and/or infection is associated with increased risk for neonatal transmission and consequent neonatal sepsis, patients are screened for GBS during early pregnancy, and again at 36 to 38 weeks' gestation. Patients who are at risk for GBS but who have unknown GBS status should receive intrapartum penicillin G to prevent neonatal transmission and infection.</p><p>Group B streptococcus (GBS) frequently colonizes the urethra, vagina, and rectum. It rarely causes infection in immunocompetent adults. In pregnancy, however, infection with GBS is common. Pregnant patients can develop asymptomatic bacteriuria, cystitis, pyelonephritis, chorioamnionitis, and endometritis linked to GBS. Maternal colonization and infection increase the risk for neonatal transmission. Therefore, pregnant patients are screened with a urine culture early in pregnancy and an anovaginal swab at 36 to 38 weeks' gestation. If either of these is positive or the patient develops a GBS infection during pregnancy, she should be treated with intrapartum antibiotic therapy during labor to prevent neonatal transmission. In cases where GBS status is unknown, patients with risk factors for GBS (including but not limited to previous history, preterm labor, preterm prelabor rupture of membranes, prolonged rupture of membranes, intrapartum fever) should receive intrapartum penicillin G as prophylaxis. This is important as GBS is a leading cause of neonatal sepsis, which is associated with significant morbidity and mortality.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, F, and G.</b></p><p>Expectant management (Choice A) would be appropriate for a term birth with no risk factors for GBS or with negative screening. This patient is in labor at 36 weeks' gestation, and her GBS status is unknown.</p><p>Liver function tests (Choice B) are appropriate in screening for preeclampsia or HELLP syndrome. They may also be appropriate in screening for maternal hepatitis. Similarly, measurement of platelet count (Choice C) is appropriate in the evaluation of HELLP syndrome, coagulopathy, disseminated intravascular coagulopathy, and many other conditions. Neither test has a role in evaluating or managing GBS colonization and prevention.</p><p>Administration of oxytocin (Choice D) is appropriate for induction of labor and management of prolonged labor. This patient has appropriate tocodynamometry readings, and her cervical change is also appropriate. There is currently no sign of failure to progress or the need for labor augmentation.</p><p>Amnioinfusion (Choice F) is the process of instilling fluid into the amniotic cavity. Amnioinfusion may be useful for the management of oligohydramnios, especially when fetal heart rate monitoring discloses variable decelerations suggestive of umbilical cord compression. For this patient, there is no indication for amnioinfusion.</p><p>Cesarean delivery (Choice G) is indicated in many circumstances, including but not limited to known cephalopelvic disproportion, placenta or vasa previa, active vaginal infection (eg, herpes), uterine rupture, failure to progress, and fetal distress. It is not indicated in the prevention of GBS infection in mothers with unknown GBS status.</p>", "23": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Active labor occurs from the time the cervix is 4 to 6 cm dilated until it is completely dilated at 10 cm. It can be prolonged, when cervical dilation proceeds at a rate less than 1 to 2 cm per hour, or arrested, when cervical dilation does not change in 4 hours with adequate contractions or 6 hours with inadequate contractions. Prolongation is managed with oxytocin augmentation, whereas arrest necessitates cesarean delivery.</p><p>Labor consists of three defined stages. The first stage is divided into latent and active labor and encompasses the start of labor (contractions and cervical change) until the completion of cervical dilation and effacement. The second stage includes the time from complete cervical dilation to the birth of the fetus. The third stage contains the delivery of the placenta. The first stage of labor is considered latent until the cervix is 4 to 6 cm dilated, after which the rate of cervical dilation increases, and the patient is considered to be in active labor. Prolongation or arrest of labor progression can occur throughout the labor process. The active stage of labor can be prolonged, which occurs when the cervix is dilating at less than 1 to 2 cm per hour. Arrest of labor occurs in patients who are dilated to at least 6 cm with no cervical change for greater than 4 hours with adequate contractions or no cervical change for greater than 6 hours with inadequate contractions. Additionally, the second stage can be prolonged, which occurs after 2 to 3 hours, or arrested if descent of the fetus fails to occur or further pushing is unsuccessful. Prolongation and arrest can be caused by hypotonic contractions, cephalopelvic disproportion, and a non-occiput anterior fetal presentation. Fetal birth weight, maternal BMI, and neuraxial anesthesia can also play a role. Prolongation of labor can be managed with oxytocin to augment uterine contractions and prostaglandins to assist in cervical ripening, whereas arrest of labor is managed with cesarean delivery. This patient has not made progress in labor for 3 hours but does not yet meet the definition of arrest of labor; therefore, she can be augmented with oxytocin.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Cesarean delivery (Choice B) is appropriate in the event of arrest of labor, and for many other indications. This patient has not yet met the definition of arrest of labor. Oxytocin is appropriate currently.</p><p>Continuous epidural analgesia (Choice C) is appropriate for maternal discomfort during labor and delivery. It is not necessarily associated with failure of labor to progress; studies on this are mixed and associations are unclear. Currently, augmentation of labor with oxytocin is most appropriate.</p><p>Manual rotation of the vertex (Choice D) is not necessary currently. This fetus has an occiput anterior presentation, which is optimal for vaginal delivery. Fetal occiput posterior presentation may benefit from manual rotation, which has been shown to prevent the need for operative delivery.</p><p>Sedation for rest and hydration by intravenous fluids (Choice E) is not necessary currently. Maternal sedation can also limit the strength of contraction and potentially worsen progression of labor.</p>", "24": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: A healthy fetal heart rate is between 110 and 160 bpm with 6 to 25 bpm of variability. An isolated variable deceleration, commonly caused by umbilical cord compression, can occur during the normal process of labor, and expectant management remains appropriate in a patient who is progressing normally with an otherwise reassuring tracing.</p><p>A healthy fetal heart rate is between 110 and 160 beats per minute (bpm) with 6 to 25 bpm of variability and spontaneous accelerations. Intermittent early and variable decelerations can also occur during the normal process of labor due to head compression and umbilical cord compression, respectively. In comparison, uteroplacental insufficiency and fetal hypoxemia can present with late decelerations on the fetal heart rate tracing, which are defined as gradual decreases in the fetal heart rate that begin after the peak of uterine contraction and nadir more than 30 seconds after the initiation of heart rate decline. This patient's tracing shows multiple contractions, along with a single deceleration that, while occurring in parallel to a contraction, is not accompanied by other similar contractions. This likely reflects a variable deceleration, commonly caused by umbilical cord compression. There are no other signs of imminent fetal distress based on this tracing, nor is there evidence of failure to progress/arrest of labor. Expectant management remains appropriate unless the tracing begins to show concerning findings such as late decelerations or fetal bradycardia.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Administration of magnesium sulfate (Choice B) is appropriate in the management of eclampsia. This patient has a reassuring blood pressure, no signs of end-organ injury, and is in active labor with appropriate frequency and strength of contractions.</p><p>Labor augmentation with oxytocin (Choice C) is appropriate in cases of prolongation of labor. This patient has had appropriate strength and frequency of contractions plus cervical change.</p><p>Forceps-assisted vaginal delivery (Choice D) is appropriate in cases of failure to progress during the second stage of labor. This involves the use of a forceps to grasp the fetal vertex, providing mechanical traction to assist maternal contractions. This patient has not yet completed the active phase of the first stage of labor.</p><p>Immediate cesarean delivery (Choice E) is appropriate in cases of late decelerations and fetal distress, among other indications. This patient has not yet completed the active phase of the first stage of labor, and the fetal heart rate tracing is reassuring.</p>", "25": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Septic pelvic thrombophlebitis is a rare puerperal complication in which thrombus in the ovarian or pelvic veins becomes infected. It presents with persistent postpartum fever unresponsive to broad-spectrum antibiotics, often with tachycardia and lower abdominal pain or uterine tenderness, and is treated with broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics combined with anticoagulation.</p><p>Septic pelvic thrombophlebitis (SPT) is a rare but serious obstetric complication, primarily occurring in the puerperium, characterized by thrombus formation within the ovarian or pelvic veins that subsequently becomes infected. Pathophysiologically, SPT involves Virchow's triad: endothelial injury, venous stasis, and hypercoagulability, all of which are accentuated during pregnancy and the postpartum period. Clinically, SPT presents with persistent postpartum fever unresponsive to broad-spectrum antibiotics, often accompanied by tachycardia and lower abdominal pain or uterine tenderness, as is seen in this patient. The condition is difficult to diagnose, as the clinical presentation can mimic other postpartum infections; thus, imaging techniques like CT scan or MRI with contrast are crucial for visualizing the thrombosed veins and confirming the diagnosis. Prevention of SPT involves minimizing risk factors through appropriate management of labor and delivery, particularly by reducing the incidence of cesarean deliveries when possible and ensuring optimal aseptic techniques. Additionally, early mobilization and the use of prophylactic anticoagulants in high-risk patients may decrease the risk for thrombus formation. The treatment of SPT involves the administration of broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics to address the infectious component, combined with anticoagulation therapy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</b></p><p>Acute salpingitis (Choice A) presents with lower abdominal pain, fever, and vaginal discharge, often as a result of a sexually transmitted infection. Salpingitis would be expected to respond to broad-spectrum antibiotics.</p><p>Aspiration pneumonia (Choice B) presents with cough, fever, and dyspnea after inhaling foreign material into the lungs. This patient's lungs are clear to auscultation, making this diagnosis unlikely.</p><p>Systemic candidiasis (Choice D) would present with fever, chills, and potentially sepsis due to fungemia. Systemic candidiasis is rare in immunocompetent patients.</p><p>Urinary tract infection (Choice E) is incorrect. This patient has a normal urinalysis and urinary tract infections in adults do not commonly present with fever, unless the infection has ascended to the kidneys, as in pyelonephritis. This patient also has no costovertebral angle tenderness.</p>", "26": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Hyperflexion of the hips during the second stage of labor can compress the femoral nerve under the inguinal ligament, causing numbness of the anterior and medial thigh and weakness of the quadriceps muscle. Compression at the fibular head in stirrups instead injures the peroneal nerve, causing footdrop.</p><p>The most likely underlying cause of this patient's neuropathy is hyperflexion of the hips during the second stage of labor. Nerve compression injuries are caused by prolonged pressure against a nerve fiber, which results in nerve ischemia and demyelination, which may or may not be reversible depending on the extent of ischemia. The most commonly injured nerves in the lithotomy position (ie, with feet in stirrups) are the femoral nerve and the peroneal nerve. The femoral nerve can be compressed under the inguinal ligament when the hip is flexed greater than 90 degrees, which can lead to numbness of the anterior and medial aspects of the thigh, as well as weakness of the quadriceps muscle, as in this patient. Recovery of the function of the nerve depends on the length of time that the nerve was exposed to ischemia, but physical therapy can be useful.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Compression of the lumbar nerve root by herniated intervertebral disc (Choice A) can present in a similar anatomic distribution to the patient, as the femoral nerve is comprised of roots L2, L3, and L4. Pre-existing herniated discs can be exacerbated by weight gain and postural shifts in pregnancy, but injury to the intervertebral discs is not a typical risk in labor and delivery.</p><p>Compression of the lumbosacral plexus by fetal vertex (Choice B), or sciatica, is a common complication of pregnancy, especially during the third trimester as the fetus grows in size. Symptoms present in the distribution of the sciatic nerve (eg, buttocks, posterior thigh, leg), and not typically in the anterior thigh.</p><p>Compression of the right lower extremity in stirrups (Choice C), specifically compression at the fibular head, may cause peroneal nerve injury, leading to footdrop and numbness over the dorsal foot and lateral lower leg. This patient has numbness and weakness over the thigh.</p><p>Epidural hematoma compressing lumbar and sacral nerve roots (Choice D) is incorrect. While epidural hematomas can present with neurologic deficits, they are usually bilateral and not within the territory of an individual nerve, as with this patient.</p>", "27": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Lochia is the normal vaginal discharge of the puerperium and may last for 4 to 6 weeks, progressing from bloody lochia rubra to lochia serosa and finally to white to yellow lochia alba. A patient whose discharge follows this course and who has no fever or other signs of infection should continue self-care of the perineum.</p><p>Vaginal discharge following delivery is termed lochia and may last during the period of the puerperium for 4 to 6 weeks. The early discharge, termed lochia rubra, contains blood and may last for up to 10 days. Lochia slowly becomes less bloody and more serous, termed lochia serosa, before finally becoming white to yellow in color, termed lochia alba. This patient's uncomplicated course, lack of fever, and description of discharge are suggestive of normal lochia rubra and serosa, and she should continue self-care of the perineum. Recommended self-care of the perineum includes sitting on soft surfaces, rinsing the area with warm water, cooling sanitary pads before using them, and sitz baths or traditional baths in a clean tub. Patients with extensive perineal lacerations or episiotomies may be advised to avoid baths for 1 to 2 weeks to avoid stinging or premature dissolution of sutures.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Refraining from taking baths for 6 to 8 weeks (Choice A) is not necessary, though patients should avoid soaking in dirty sources of water like lakes, pools, and hot tubs. Patients with perineal tears or episiotomies may be advised to avoid baths in the acute postpartum period, but not for 6 to 8 weeks.</p><p>Oral antibiotic therapy (Choice C) is indicated in minor postpartum infections, such as episiotomy infection, which may present with pain, erythema, and purulent discharge from the perineum. Puerperal sepsis, which typically presents with fever, abdominal pain, mucopurulent vaginal discharge, or septic shock, requires hospital admission for intravenous antibiotics. This patient's discharge is consistent with normal lochia, and she has no signs or symptoms of infection.</p><p>Oral ergometrine therapy (Choice D), an ergot alkaloid, may be useful for the treatment of postpartum hemorrhage secondary to uterine atony. Postpartum hemorrhage secondary to uterine atony presents with significant bleeding and potential hemodynamic instability. Common causes of postpartum hemorrhage include uterine atony; lacerations or trauma to the uterus, cervix, vagina, or introitus; retained products of conception; and coagulopathy. This patient's discharge is consistent with normal lochia rubra and serosa.</p><p>Suction curettage (Choice E) is useful for managing postpartum hemorrhage secondary to retained placental fragments. Retained placental fragments present with significant vaginal bleeding after delivery. This patient's discharge has become progressively less bloody, which is consistent with normal lochia.</p>", "28": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Small bowel obstruction (SBO) occurs from partial or complete blockage of the small bowel lumen, and typically presents with nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, and abdominal distention. SBO has many potential causes, although the most common are postsurgical peritoneal adhesions and hernias. Abdominal x-rays typically show dilated small bowel loops.</p><p>The most likely cause of this patient's abdominal pain and abnormal bowel sounds in the setting of pregnancy and prior appendectomy is small bowel obstruction (SBO). Adhesions are the most common cause of SBO in a patient with a history of prior intra-abdominal surgery. SBO occurs from partial or complete blockage of the small bowel lumen, and typically presents with nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, and abdominal distention. SBO has many potential causes, although the most common are postsurgical peritoneal adhesions and hernias. On physical examination, SBO typically presents with diffuse abdominal tenderness, distention, tympany to percussion, and abnormal bowel sounds. Potential complications of SBO include bowel rupture or necrosis, which can result in abrupt peritonitic abdominal signs such as rebound tenderness and guarding. Abdominal x-rays, if obtained in the upright position, typically show multiple air-fluid levels in the setting of dilated small bowel loops. Obtaining an x-ray of the abdomen is a reasonable first step in the evaluation of potential SBO in a pregnant patient as it limits radiation risk and avoids unnecessary surgical risk. Management requires bowel rest, intravenous hydration, and surgical intervention. For cases of persistent, nonresolving, or complete obstruction, exploratory laparotomy should be performed to alleviate the cause of the obstruction.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Measurement of serum CA 125 concentration (Choice A) is typically performed if an ovarian malignancy is suspected. It is not a diagnostic test of choice in suspected bowel obstruction. This patient, aged 32 years and with good health without family history, would be unlikely to have an ovarian malignancy.</p><p>Colonoscopy (Choice C) is useful in the diagnosis and management of colorectal pathology such as malignancy, inflammatory bowel disease, arteriovenous malformation, and strictures. It does not have an immediate role in the diagnosis or management of SBO.</p><p>Esophagogastroduodenoscopy (Choice D) is used in the diagnosis and management of upper gastrointestinal pathology such as esophagitis, esophageal varices, gastritis, peptic ulcer disease, malignancy, and proximal small bowel or solid-organ pathology such as choledocholithiasis. While this patient has risk factors for choledocholithiasis given pregnancy, her examination is more consistent with bowel obstruction.</p><p>Laparoscopy (Choice E) describes surgical visualization of the peritoneal cavity and contents and may be diagnostic or therapeutic depending on indication. For this patient, prior to establishing a diagnosis, the associated surgical risk and morbidity in pregnancy would be inappropriately high.</p>", "29": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Carpal tunnel syndrome results from compression of the median nerve within the carpal tunnel and presents with pain and paresthesia in the median nerve distribution and weakness of thumb abduction. Pregnancy is a known risk factor, and because symptoms often resolve after delivery, wrist splints are the most appropriate initial management in pregnancy.</p><p>Carpal tunnel syndrome (CTS) results from compression of the median nerve within the carpal tunnel of the wrist. The carpal tunnel is located anterior to the carpal bones and is bound by the flexor retinaculum. The flexor tendons of the fingers course through the tunnel, along with the median nerve. The space is rigid and does not generally permit compression; thus, any inflammatory, traumatic, or space-filling lesion may result in CTS. Patients generally present with pain and paresthesia in the median nerve dermatomal distribution, along with weakness with grip strength or thumb abduction, as seen in this patient. Pregnancy is a known risk factor for CTS, which is hypothesized to result from fluid retention that expands the synovium within the tunnel, leading to nerve compression. CTS is frequently associated with hypothyroidism, rheumatoid arthritis, acromegaly, amyloidosis, ganglion cysts, obesity, and acute trauma to the wrist. Conservative management should be pursued in pregnancy with the use of wrist splints, especially overnight, as CTS often resolves after delivery of the newborn. If symptoms remain severe, patients may pursue corticosteroid injections or carpal tunnel release when indicated.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Corticosteroid injection near the carpal tunnel (Choice A) may be a reasonable treatment option after a trial of using wrist splints.</p><p>Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) therapy (Choice B) has not been shown to be more effective than placebo in improving CTS symptoms. Additionally, NSAIDs should be avoided in pregnancy.</p><p>Operative carpal tunnel release (Choice C) may be a reasonable treatment option in severe CTS when conservative management with wrist splints and corticosteroid injections has failed to improve symptoms. Most cases of pregnancy-induced CTS will resolve with time after delivery of the newborn.</p><p>Observation until delivery of the newborn (Choice E) may be a reasonable treatment option in mild cases of CTS, as symptoms often resolve after delivery of the newborn. For this patient experiencing more severe pain, the use of wrist splints would be the most appropriate next step in management.</p>", "30": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Fat necrosis can cause a firm breast mass in an area of blunt breast trauma or following a surgical procedure, and it may show calcifications on imaging. It is a benign condition that is not associated with lymphadenopathy or skin changes, and surgical excision is not generally necessary.</p><p>Fat necrosis can cause breast masses in areas of blunt breast trauma, as seen in this patient, or following a surgical procedure. Patients may not report a history of trauma, as inciting injuries may be minor and seem insignificant to the patient. Possible features include erythema, irregularity, nodularity, and calcifications on imaging. Once they appear, these lesions do not typically fluctuate in size over time. While fat necrosis can appear malignant on ultrasonography, it is a benign condition that shows macroscopic fat on mammography and is not associated with lymphadenopathy or skin changes. Treatment with surgical excision is not generally necessary for fat necrosis but can be offered in cases of pain or distortion of the breast. If the excised lesion is large, the patient may benefit from fat grafting or adjacent tissue rearrangement to prevent contour deformity.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Duct ectasia (Choice A) more often occurs in patients who are perimenopausal and is a widening of the lactiferous ducts with thickening of their walls, often near the nipple. Ectatic ducts are often asymptomatic until they become blocked, at which time they may become nodular or painful.</p><p>Fibroadenoma (Choice C) is one of the most common benign breast masses in patients younger than 35 years. It typically presents as a unilateral, well-circumscribed, solid, mobile, rubbery mass that has an increase in size in relation to the menstrual cycle or during pregnancy secondary to increased estrogen concentrations. The features of this patient's mass are more consistent with fat necrosis.</p><p>Intraductal papilloma (Choice D) is a benign tumor that originates from the walls of lactiferous ducts and proliferates into the ductal lumen. Because of their origin, intraductal papillomas are the most common cause of unilateral bloody nipple discharge. They are usually too small to be palpated externally, unlike the mass in this patient.</p><p>Sclerosing adenosis (Choice E) is a benign lesion most commonly noted on mammography or found microscopically in a breast biopsy, most commonly in patients who are perimenopausal. It can also present as a component of fibrocystic change. Presentation as a large, palpable mass is uncommon.</p>", "31": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Fibrocystic changes of the breast occur most commonly in patients who are premenopausal and present with breast pain and lumps, which may be bilateral and are believed to occur in response to hormonal fluctuations during the menstrual cycle. The condition is benign, and re-examination after menses, when the changes should subside, can help with diagnosis.</p><p>Fibrocystic changes of the breast occur most commonly in patients who are premenopausal. Patients may present with various sites of breast pain and lumps, which may be bilateral. On examination, the cysts may appear blue. The condition is characterized by the presence of cysts, stromal fibrosis, and apocrine metaplasia on histology, and is believed to occur in response to hormonal fluctuations during the menstrual cycle. Re-examination after menses may be helpful for diagnosis because the size of fibrocystic changes should subside after menses, distinguishing it from other conditions. Fibrocystic change is a benign condition and does not generally convey an increased risk for cancer, with the exception of certain subtypes, but it can result in recurrent mastalgia. If there is associated discomfort, aspiration can be performed, which may relieve symptoms. Cyst aspiration can yield fluid that is clear, milky, yellow, green, or brown, which are all benign. Care is supportive and includes NSAIDs and a well-fitting brassiere.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Breast abscess (Choice A) is often a complication of mastitis, most commonly occurring in patients who are breast-feeding. It typically presents with a localized region of indurated, fluctuant, erythematous, and tender tissue, with or without fever. Cyclic bilateral symptoms are more consistent with fibrocystic changes.</p><p>Breast engorgement (Choice B) occurs when there has been an increase in breast milk production in relation to the amount that is released. This can cause bilateral, painful swelling of the breasts and may present with fever. This patient is not currently breast-feeding.</p><p>Fibroadenoma (Choice C) is one of the most common benign breast masses in patients younger than 35 years. It typically presents as a unilateral, well-circumscribed, solid, mobile, rubbery mass that increases in size in relation to the menstrual cycle or during pregnancy secondary to increased estrogen concentrations. This patient has no distinct masses on examination.</p><p>Mastitis (Choice E) presents with erythema and induration in an area of tender, warm skin. It typically occurs in breast-feeding patients as a consequence of microtrauma to the nipple leading to inoculation from cutaneous or oral flora. Staphylococcus aureus is a common pathogen. It does not demonstrate a cyclical timeline related to menses.</p>", "32": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: A palpable breast mass in a patient who is perimenopausal requires evaluation to confirm or rule out malignancy. Imaging with mammography, or ultrasonography in patients younger than 35 years, is part of that evaluation, but a mass suggestive of breast cancer should undergo core-needle or excisional biopsy rather than be followed with repeat imaging.</p><p>Breast cancer encompasses many types of breast carcinoma, including invasive ductal and lobular carcinoma, medullary carcinoma, inflammatory breast carcinoma, and cystosarcoma phyllodes. The most common type of breast cancer is invasive ductal carcinoma, which typically presents as a palpable, immobile breast mass, which may show features such as spiculated margins and microcalcifications on mammography. Ductal carcinoma can be associated with pathologic nipple discharge. Evaluation of a breast mass first involves imaging with either mammography (if the patient is older than 35 years) or ultrasonography (if the patient is younger than 35 years), due to decreasing density of breast tissue with age. If the palpable mass has features suggestive of breast cancer, further evaluation with a core-needle or excisional biopsy is the most appropriate next step. Additionally, this patient is experiencing perimenopause, which can cause benign breast changes, making biopsy appropriate to rule out or confirm malignancy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Testing for BRCA mutations (Choice A) is appropriate in breast cancer patients with a strong family history of breast or ovarian cancer, especially in a first-degree relative, such as a parent. However, it is not a diagnostic test of active malignancy, and this patient should be diagnosed before pursuing other tests.</p><p>Ductography of the left breast (Choice B) can be used in combination with mammography and/or ultrasonography to diagnose the cause of pathologic nipple discharge. Intraductal papillomas are the most common cause of unilateral bloody nipple discharge, which may reflect malignant pathology if present in the setting of a palpable breast mass. However, this patient has no history of nipple discharge.</p><p>Follow-up examination and repeat mammography in 6 months (Choice C) is inappropriate. This patient has a palpable mass, and its presence in a patient who is perimenopausal should prompt additional evaluation now. While obtaining baseline mammography is indicated, urgent biopsy is most appropriate to evaluate for breast carcinoma.</p><p>MRI of the breasts (Choice D) is used for screening in high-risk, young patients and for clarifying equivocal findings on conventional imaging. This patient already has a known, palpable mass, which should be evaluated with biopsy rather than with additional imaging.</p>", "33": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Endomyometritis is characterized by inflammation/infection of the uterine wall and causes fever, abdominal pain, abnormal uterine bleeding, tenderness of the uterine fundus, and mucopurulent vaginal discharge. In the postpartum setting, it is often preceded by prolonged rupture of membranes and/or chorioamnionitis.</p><p>This patient, who is recently postpartum with prolonged rupture of membranes and peripartum fever, now presents with recurrent fever and uterine tenderness. This is consistent with postpartum endomyometritis, which is an acute (typically) polymicrobial infection of the uterine endomyometrium involving a mixture of aerobes and anaerobes from the genital tract. During labor and delivery, microbes from the genital tract can enter the uterine cavity and result in postpartum infection. Patients typically present with fever, abdominal pain, abnormal uterine bleeding, tenderness of the uterine fundus, and mucopurulent vaginal discharge. Risk factors include premature or prolonged rupture of membranes, prolonged labor, cesarean delivery, bacterial vaginosis, group B streptococcal colonization, and manual removal of the placenta. Treatment requires broad-spectrum antibiotics, such as clindamycin and gentamicin, for appropriate antimicrobial coverage.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Chorioamnionitis (Choice A) is a bacterial infection of the fetal membranes and commonly occurs in the setting of premature or prolonged rupture of the membranes. Maternal fever and leukocytosis are characteristic, and the uterus may be tender to palpation. This patient who is recently postpartum likely had chorioamnionitis at the start of her presentation as a result of prolonged rupture of membranes. Chorioamnionitis can precede endomyometritis and presents similarly, with fever, uterine tenderness, and mucopurulent vaginal discharge.</p><p>Pelvic intra-abdominal abscess (Choice C) presents with fever, and pelvic pain and tenderness. It may occur in the postoperative or postpartum setting or may occur independently of surgery or delivery in the setting of pelvic inflammatory disease or from a gastrointestinal source (eg, ruptured appendix). It presents with abdominal pain and fever. This patient's preceding history of chorioamnionitis and characteristic presentation make endomyometritis the most likely diagnosis.</p><p>Pyelonephritis (Choice D) is a urinary tract infection that has spread to the kidneys, which is characterized by flank pain, fever and chills, nausea, vomiting, and costovertebral angle tenderness on physical examination. This patient lacks these signs and symptoms, and her preceding peripartum fever and uterine tenderness make uterine infection more likely.</p><p>Septic pelvic thrombophlebitis (Choice E) is a rare sequela of endometritis that can lead to local invasion and infection of the pelvic vein walls, which causes intimal damage and thrombosis. This condition typically presents with pelvic pain and fever, which is often widely variable in course, ranging from normal temperature to extremely high. Diagnosis typically occurs through blood cultures and imaging.</p>", "34": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Pelvic inflammatory disease results from ascending migration of a bacterial infection from the lower genital tract through the cervix and uterus to the fallopian tubes. It presents with fever, abdominopelvic pain, and mucopurulent discharge, along with cervical motion and uterine or adnexal tenderness, and it can lead to tubo-ovarian abscess, Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome, and infertility from tubal occlusion.</p><p>This otherwise healthy patient having unprotected intercourse with a new partner followed by fever, uterine and adnexal tenderness, and cervical discharge likely has pelvic inflammatory disease (PID). PID is characterized by inflammation of the upper genital tract and pelvis from ascending migration of a bacterial infection from the lower genital tract. Its clinical manifestations include fever, abdominopelvic pain, and mucopurulent vaginal discharge, as well as cervical motion and uterine or adnexal tenderness on examination. Risk factors for PID include multiple sexual partners, failure to use a barrier method of contraception, and young age. Complications of PID include Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome (ie, acute perihepatitis), tubo-ovarian abscess, and chronic issues with infertility as a result of tubal occlusion. The fallopian tube is commonly infected in PID as the infection ascends from the vagina through the cervix, uterus, and fallopian tubes.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Appendix (Choice A), when infected, presents with fever, periumbilical migrating to right lower quadrant pain, lack of appetite, and may present with signs of peritonitis if rupture has occurred. This patient has purulent cervical discharge, making a urogenital infection more likely.</p><p>Bartholin gland (Choice B), when infected, presents with pain around the lateral sides of the introitus with swelling and erythema on examination. Speculum examination would show normal internal anatomy and uterine or adnexal tenderness would not be present.</p><p>Bladder (Choice C) infection presents with dysuria, frequency, urgency, and suprapubic discomfort. This patient presents with mild pain with urination, without any other characteristic symptoms.</p><p>Vagina (Choice E) infections are often caused by yeast, or other bacterial organisms, such as Trichomonas vaginalis. Vaginitis presents with foul-smelling vaginal discharge, vaginal pruritus, and irritation, but it would not cause the uterine and adnexal tenderness seen in this patient.</p>", "35": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The USPSTF recommends testing for Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae in all sexually active patients younger than 25 years, as both infections can be asymptomatic and can lead to pelvic inflammatory disease, tubo-ovarian abscess, and infertility. Diagnosis is made with a nucleic acid amplification test.</p><p>Testing for Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae is recommended by the United States Preventive Services Task Force (USPSTF) for all sexually active patients younger than 25 years. C. trachomatis is a sexually transmitted infection that typically presents with urethritis, dysuria, dyspareunia, and urethral or vaginal discharge. N. gonorrhoeae is also a sexually transmitted infection that presents most commonly with mucopurulent discharge and pruritus. However, both infections can also be asymptomatic, thus the recommendation for screening. Complications include cervicitis, pelvic inflammatory disease, and tubo-ovarian abscess, as well as long-term issues with fertility. Diagnosis is made with a nucleic acid amplification test, and treatment consists of either azithromycin or doxycycline for chlamydia and ceftriaxone for gonorrhea.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Annual Pap smear screening (Choice A) is incorrect. Annual Pap smears are appropriate in patients with high-grade cervical intraepithelial neoplasia, not in asymptomatic patients with no significant prior history. This patient should receive Pap smears on a standard schedule for her age, along with vaccination against human papillomavirus (HPV). Pap smears are recommended to start in patients aged 21 years and be repeated every 3 years until the age of 30 years. For patients aged 30 to 65 years, the frequency can be reduced to every 5 years if done in conjunction with HPV co-testing and both results are normal.</p><p>Human papillomavirus vaccination at the age of 26 years (Choice C) is not recommended. According to CDC guidelines, the HPV vaccination is recommended for persons aged 9 to 26 years. Ideally, the patient should receive the vaccination prior to beginning sexual activity; otherwise, it should be administered as promptly as reasonable. Delaying until age 26 years would present unnecessary risk.</p><p>Measurement of serum thyroid-stimulating hormone concentration (Choice D) is appropriate in the evaluation of potential hypo- or hyperthyroidism. Screening recommendations vary. As of 2024, the USPSTF guidelines are inconclusive as to the balance of benefits and risks, citing insufficient evidence in nonpregnant, asymptomatic adults, such as this patient.</p><p>Pneumococcal vaccination at this visit (Choice E) is incorrect. Pneumococcal vaccination is recommended for children younger than 5 years, those younger than 18 years with high-risk medical conditions, adults aged 19 to 49 years with certain chronic medical conditions or immunocompromise, and all adults aged 50 years and older. This healthy 19-year-old patient has no indication for pneumococcal vaccination.</p>", "36": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: After sexual assault, patients should receive a nonjudgmental history and physical examination, collection of evidence, and prophylaxis against sexually transmitted infections and pregnancy. Prophylaxis against Neisseria gonorrhoeae is intramuscular ceftriaxone, given along with doxycycline or azithromycin, metronidazole, and HIV postexposure prophylaxis.</p><p>In cases of alleged sexual assault, patients should receive a thorough, objective, nonjudgmental history and physical examination including collection of evidence based on appropriate local laws/statutes. Important prophylactic considerations include sexually transmitted infection prophylaxis against common pathogens, such as Neisseria gonorrhoeae, Chlamydia trachomatis, Trichomonas vaginalis, hepatitis (if the patient was not previously vaccinated), and HIV. Pregnancy prophylaxis should also be offered. Patients should be referred to local social and psychological support resources such as assault survivor groups. Safe disposition should be considered, as the alleged assailant and the patient may be known to each other or living in proximity. For adult, capacitated patients, the opportunity to make a police report should be offered. In cases of alleged assault of a minor, older person, or vulnerable individual, reporting is mandatory. This patient should receive prophylaxis against N. gonorrhoeae with intramuscular ceftriaxone, which is used to treat bacterial infections. She should also be given doxycycline or azithromycin, metronidazole, HIV postexposure prophylaxis, and pregnancy prophylaxis at her discretion.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Intramuscular immune globulin (Choice B) refers to a broad class of medications that may prevent certain infections, including but not limited to tetanus, rabies, and hepatitis. In the case of a previously vaccinated patient, there is no role for immune globulin after alleged sexual assault.</p><p>Oral fluconazole (Choice C) is appropriate in the treatment of yeast infections. Yeast infections are not transmitted through sex, and if the patient were to develop one in the following weeks, she should be evaluated and treated on an as-needed basis.</p><p>Oral penicillin (Choice D) would be duplicative in the setting of intramuscular ceftriaxone, as ceftriaxone covers the same spectrum of sexually transmitted infections as penicillin (eg, syphilis), while also covering gonorrhea, making it the most appropriate treatment.</p><p>Oral trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (Choice E) is used in the management of cellulitis and urinary tract infections, among other infectious conditions. It is not used in the prevention of common sexually transmitted infections.</p>", "37": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: A Pap smear showing a high-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion carries an increased risk for progression to cervical cancer and is evaluated by colposcopy, which allows visual assessment and biopsy of abnormal lesions. Excision with a loop electrosurgical excision procedure or cone biopsy may follow colposcopy but does not replace it.</p><p>Carcinoma of the cervix is typically associated with infection from human papillomavirus (HPV) high-risk strains 16, 18, 31, and 33. Risk factors include multiple sexual partners, smoking, and immunosuppression. Cervical cancer screening with Pap smears is recommended every 3 years for those aged 21 to 29 years and every 5 years in conjunction with HPV co-testing (every 3 if co-testing is not performed) in patients aged 30 to 65 years. The results of these screening tests then determine the need for further evaluation and diagnosis. The cytology results may be normal, indeterminate, or show signs of atypical cells (ie, atypical squamous cells of undetermined significance) or precancerous lesions (low- or high-grade intraepithelial lesions). High-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion (HSIL), as seen in this patient, has an increased risk for progression to cervical cancer. Abnormal Pap smear cytology results are evaluated by colposcopy, which allows for visual assessment and biopsy of abnormal lesions and is the most appropriate next step in management of this patient. High-grade lesions on cytology may be treated with excision via loop electrosurgical excision procedure or a cone biopsy of the cervix.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Human papillomavirus testing (Choice A) is appropriate in conjunction with Pap smears every 5 years for patients aged 30 to 65 years. This patient already has HSIL, and HPV testing is less meaningful in such cases. She requires evaluation via colposcopy followed by excision of any identified lesions.</p><p>Repeat Pap smear (Choice B) could be appropriate in cases of indeterminate results. This patient had a Pap smear identifying HSIL, which requires colposcopy.</p><p>Administration of human papillomavirus vaccine (Choice C) is incorrect. According to CDC guidelines, the HPV vaccination is recommended for persons aged 9 to 26 years. Select patients aged 27 to 45 years may receive it if the benefits are felt to outweigh the risks; however, this patient already has HSIL, a neoplastic lesion within the cervix. Management of HSIL to prevent progression to malignancy is critical.</p><p>Loop electrosurgical excision of the cervical transformation zone (Choice E) is incorrect. While it may be an appropriate procedure to remove abnormal lesions, it occurs during or after colposcopy. The most appropriate next step for this patient who has received abnormal cytology results is evaluation via colposcopy to identify and assess the lesions.</p>", "38": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Uterine sarcoma typically presents with a rapidly enlarging pelvic mass, abdominal pain, bloating, and postmenopausal bleeding. Physical examination will disclose an enlarged uterus, and ultrasonography will show a heterogeneous mass with possible foci of internal necrosis. Risk factors include history of pelvic radiation, retinoblastoma, and long-term use of tamoxifen.</p><p>Uterine sarcoma is a rare malignant proliferation of the myometrium or the stroma of the uterus that most commonly occurs in a patient who is postmenopausal and typically presents with postmenopausal bleeding, abdominal pain, bloating, and a rapidly enlarging pelvic mass. Physical examination will disclose an enlarged uterus, and ultrasonography will show a heterogeneous mass with possible foci of internal necrosis. Typical evaluation includes an endometrial biopsy, as well as a biopsy of the mass itself, while treatment involves a total hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy, as well as lymph node dissection, and neoadjuvant chemotherapy depending on the extent of disease. Risk factors for the development of uterine sarcoma include pelvic radiation therapy, a history of retinoblastoma, and long-term use of tamoxifen.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Adenomyosis (Choice A) describes the growth of endometrial glands within the myometrium and is commonly seen in multigravid patients. It presents with menorrhagia and an enlarged, boggy uterus, not with distinctive masses.</p><p>Cervical cancer (Choice B) presents with vaginal bleeding, typically postcoital, but can be asymptomatic. Risk factors include smoking, multiple sexual partners, immunocompromise, and high-risk strains of human papillomavirus. Physical examination will show an exophytic lesion of the cervix, not masses in the myometrium as seen in this patient.</p><p>Colon cancer (Choice C) typically presents with occult bleeding in the stool and iron deficiency anemia. Symptoms can also include weight loss, tenesmus, constipation, narrow stools, and large bowel obstruction. The primary lesion would not be found outside the colon, and metastases are typically found in the liver, lungs, and brain, not within the myometrium.</p><p>Endometriosis (Choice D) presents with chronic dyspareunia, dysmenorrhea, infertility, and dyschezia as a result of the presence of ectopic endometrial tissue outside the uterus. It does not present as a large mass in the myometrium. Endometrial implants may not be visible on imaging, and definitive diagnosis is made with diagnostic laparoscopy.</p><p>Leiomyomata uteri (Choice E), or uterine fibroids, are benign neoplasms of the uterine myometrium that present with vaginal bleeding, pelvic pain, and miscarriage or infertility. They are estrogen responsive, causing increases in size with pregnancy and decreases in size with menopause. Postmenopausal presentation is not consistent with this diagnosis, and enlarged regional lymphadenopathy is more consistent with a malignant neoplasm.</p>", "39": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Vulvar carcinoma, most often squamous cell carcinoma, presents most commonly as a lesion of the labia majora in patients aged 55 to 85 years and may be pruritic, friable, and painful. Risk factors include human papillomavirus infection, smoking, immunosuppression, chronic inflammation, and a history of cervical malignancy, and diagnosis is made with a biopsy of the lesion.</p><p>This 67-year-old patient is presenting with a large, exophytic vulvar mass, raising concern for vulvar carcinoma. The most frequent underlying histopathology of vulvar carcinoma is squamous cell carcinoma. Vulvar carcinoma presents most commonly with a lesion of the labia majora in patients aged 55 to 85 years. While the lesion is commonly a nodule, papule, or plaque that may be otherwise asymptomatic, the lesion can also be pruritic and friable, leading to ulceration, bleeding, and pain. In rare instances, such as seen in this patient, squamous cell carcinoma of the vulva can present as verrucous carcinoma with a large, cauliflower-like growth that can be locally destructive. In addition to the culprit lesion, physical examination may disclose inguinal lymphadenopathy. Risk factors include infection with human papillomavirus, cigarette smoking, immunosuppression (including from diabetes mellitus), chronic inflammation (eg, lichen sclerosus), and a history of cervical malignancy. Diagnosis is made with a biopsy of the lesion. In patients who have a cervix, evaluation should include cervical cancer screening, as concurrent cervical carcinoma is common, and abdominopelvic imaging to determine extent of disease. Treatment includes surgical excision and chemoradiation.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Granuloma inguinale (Choice A), or donovanosis, is caused by Klebsiella granulomatis. It presents as a red, friable ulcer and induces additional ulcers on oppositional skin. It is painless.</p><p>Herpes simplex (Choice B) genital infection is characterized by painful vesicles in the genital area (eg, vulva, vagina, cervix, perineum). Infection typically begins with the formation of vesicles, which lyse and progress to shallow, painful ulcers with crusting and an erythematous border. A large vulvar mass is not consistent with this infection.</p><p>Metastatic cervical cancer (Choice C) most often metastasizes to the lungs, bones, and liver. Cutaneous metastasis is rare and usually manifests as nodules, papules, plaques, a maculopapular rash, or a diffuse inflammatory rash. A single large mass would be an extremely uncommon presentation, and a primary malignancy is more likely.</p><p>Vulvar abscesses (Choice E) present as an erythematous, edematous, tender, cystic mass of the vulva, and can be complicated by necrotizing fasciitis if left untreated. The mass noted in this example is not consistent with an abscess and has been present for 1 year. The patient has no systemic signs of untreated infection.</p>", "40": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The United States Preventive Services Task Force does not currently recommend routine screening for ovarian cancer in asymptomatic patients. Ovarian cancer commonly presents indolently with abdominal distention, early satiety, and weight loss in patients who are postmenopausal. Signs and symptoms may include bowel obstruction with nausea and vomiting, ascites, pleural effusions, and venous thromboembolism.</p><p>The United States Preventive Services Task Force does not currently recommend routine screening for ovarian cancer in asymptomatic patients. This patient should receive routine health maintenance examinations and should receive appropriate diagnostic testing as guided by her signs, symptoms, and physical examination. Ovarian cancer commonly presents indolently with abdominal distention, early satiety, and weight loss in patients who are postmenopausal. Signs and symptoms may include bowel obstruction with nausea and vomiting, ascites, pleural effusions, and venous thromboembolism. Physical examination may disclose a palpable adnexal mass on bimanual examination or lymphadenopathy. Ultrasonography is recommended to identify or characterize the responsible lesion, with noteworthy features including septations, cystic components, blood flow, and a heterogeneous structure. Further evaluation is completed with tumor markers, measurement of serum cancer antigen 125 (CA 125), and additional imaging to determine the extent of disease. Risk factors for the development of ovarian cancer include family history, genetic mutation (eg, BRCA), older age, smoking, nulliparity, and pelvic radiation. Treatment includes surgical removal and chemotherapy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Measurement of serum CA 125 concentration only (Choice B) and measurement of serum CA 125 concentration and pelvic ultrasonography (Choice E) could be correct in cases of known or suspected ovarian cancer. CA 125 is used as a tumor marker to trend the clinical progression of ovarian cancer and to assess for recurrence. It is not used as a screening measure of disease.</p><p>Abdominal ultrasonography (Choice C) and transvaginal ultrasonography (Choice D) are used in the evaluation of multiple abdominopelvic pathologies, including but not limited to ovarian torsion, ovarian cyst, ovarian malignancy, vaginal bleeding, pregnancy, abnormal uterine bleeding, nephrolithiasis, and appendicitis. They are not used to screen for ovarian cancer, though they may be used for case finding and diagnosis in cases of suspected ovarian cancer.</p>", "41": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Hysterosalpingography is a radiographic technique used to determine the structure of the uterine cavity and fallopian tubes, including tube patency. It is useful in the evaluation of recurrent pregnancy loss and infertility.</p><p>Hysterosalpingography is a radiographic technique used to determine the structure of the uterine cavity and fallopian tubes, including tube patency. It is useful in the evaluation of recurrent pregnancy loss and infertility. Infertility may be caused by diminished ovarian reserve, anovulation, fallopian tube occlusion, sperm hypo- or immotility, inability of sperm to penetrate the corona radiata of the ovum, structural abnormalities of the uterus, and thyroid disorders, among other causes. For a patient who has frequent unprotected sexual intercourse and has not conceived for a period of 1 year, an evaluation for underlying causes of infertility should occur. A reasonable next diagnostic step for this otherwise healthy 25-year-old patient with a history of prior ectopic pregnancy includes hysterosalpingography to assess for tubal occlusion. Prior ectopic pregnancy suggests that the patient may be at risk for abnormal embryonic implantation in an extrauterine location, potentially from prior pelvic inflammatory disease.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>CT scan of the abdomen (Choice A) is used in the evaluation of many intra-abdominal and pelvic pathologies, including but not limited to malignancy, bowel obstruction, peritonitis, appendicitis, kidney stones, and multiple infectious and inflammatory processes. It does not generally offer meaningful information as to the cause of infertility as it cannot be used to assess hormonal abnormalities or uterine/tubal patency, two common causes of infertility. Similarly, pelvic ultrasonography (Choice C) is used in the evaluation of multiple pelvic pathologies, including but not limited to ovarian and uterine malignancy, pelvic inflammatory disease, and ectopic pregnancy. It is not the initial step in the evaluation of infertility.</p><p>Endometrial biopsy (Choice D) and ovarian biopsy (Choice E) are used in suspected endometrial and ovarian malignancy, respectively. They are not the first step in the evaluation of suspected infertility.</p>", "42": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Vaginal atrophy presents with dyspareunia, postcoital bleeding, frequent urinary tract infections, and vaginal itching, among other symptoms. Examination may show vaginal dryness, loss of rugae, erythema, and bleeding with minimally traumatic contact. The diagnosis is clinical and commonly occurs in patients who are postmenopausal.</p><p>This patient, presenting with dyspareunia and postcoital bleeding, is likely experiencing vaginal atrophy, a common effect of menopause. Because of loss of hormonal stimulation of vaginal tissue, the vaginal tissue can atrophy, which presents with symptoms of dyspareunia, postcoital bleeding, frequent urinary tract infections, and vaginal itching, among other symptoms. Examination may show vaginal dryness, loss of rugae, erythema, and bleeding with minimally traumatic contact. The diagnosis is clinical and commonly occurs in patients who are postmenopausal. Treatment can include hormone replacement therapy via either systemic or topical administration, and application of vaginal lubricants to improve dryness and dyspareunia. Vaginal dilators are used to stretch the vaginal tissue to improve symptoms of dyspareunia.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Cervical polyp (Choice A) presents with bleeding, which may be postcoital or during menses. However, a cervical polyp would not present with an erythematous vagina that bleeds during examination.</p><p>Cystitis (Choice B) presents with suprapubic pain, dysuria, urinary frequency, and urinary urgency. Patients may experience fever. Dyspareunia and postcoital bleeding would not be typical symptoms of uncomplicated, infectious cystitis.</p><p>Degenerating leiomyomata uteri (Choice C) may present with an irregularly shaped uterus but would not present with vaginal erythema and bleeding.</p><p>Uterine prolapse (Choice D) presents with displacement of the uterus into the vagina, which would be visible on examination. Vaginal bleeding would not be expected, though uterine bleeding may occur.</p>", "43": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Premature ovarian failure presents in women of reproductive age with anovulation, amenorrhea, and infertility, as well as signs of early menopause, such as night sweats, hot flashes, and vaginal atrophy. Laboratory studies show decreased estrogen with increased follicle-stimulating hormone and luteinizing hormone concentrations.</p><p>Premature ovarian failure, also known as autoimmune ovarian failure or primary ovarian insufficiency, classically presents in a woman of reproductive age with anovulation, amenorrhea, and infertility. It can also present with signs of early menopause, such as hot flashes, night sweats, and vaginal atrophy. It is caused by autoimmune inflammation and destruction within the ovaries, leading to a decrease in estrogen production, limited follicle reserve, and loss of ovarian stromal cells. Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and luteinizing hormone (LH) are appropriately increased due to the lack of negative feedback on the anterior pituitary. Premature ovarian failure is most often idiopathic but is more common in patients with karyotype abnormalities or other autoimmune diseases, such as chronic lymphocytic (Hashimoto) thyroiditis. It can be evaluated via a progesterone stimulation test.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Hypothalamic amenorrhea (Choice A), also known as functional hypothalamic amenorrhea, occurs in the setting of increased stress or decreased energy availability, which may be secondary to inadequate nutrition or overexercising. This patient has a normal BMI and exercises at a nonexcessive level for most runners. Concentrations of FSH and LH will be decreased as a result of irregular gonadotropin-releasing hormone secretion.</p><p>Male factor (Choice B) infertility refers to many potential causes of qualitative or quantitative semen abnormalities. This patient's partner has a child from a previous relationship, and her menstrual irregularities and abnormal hormone concentrations argue for a female cause of infertility.</p><p>Pelvic adhesions (Choice C) are a common cause of infertility in patients with a history of pelvic inflammatory disease. This patient has no history of gynecologic illness and has abnormal hormone concentrations that would be normal in the setting of pelvic adhesions.</p><p>Pituitary adenoma (Choice D) may present with many symptoms depending on the presence or absence of mass effect on surrounding structures (eg, optic chiasm) and the presence or absence of functional hormone production. Adenomas usually only produce one type of hormone, and this patient lacks headache or any symptoms of a structural lesion.</p>", "44": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome is an exaggerated follicular response to injected gonadotropins commonly used in infertility treatment. Patients present with a spectrum of symptoms, including abdominal bloating, nausea, vomiting, and pain depending on the severity. Ultrasonography may show free pelvic fluid and ovaries with multiple simultaneously developing follicles, which will appear as cystic structures.</p><p>Ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome is an exaggerated follicular response to injected gonadotropins commonly used in infertility treatment. Patients present with a spectrum of symptoms, including mild to severe nausea and vomiting, abdominal pain and distention, fluid retention/edema, shortness of breath, and thrombosis. Diagnosis is suspected clinically and confirmed through ultrasonography showing enlarged ovaries containing multiple developing follicles and cysts, along with free pelvic fluid. Treatment involves supportive care plus hormonal discontinuation in moderate to severe cases. Paracentesis may be required in cases of severe ascites. Complications include ovarian torsion, acute respiratory distress syndrome, acute kidney injury, thromboembolism, and electrolyte imbalances, among others.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, E, F, H, I, and J.</b></p><p>Corpus luteum cyst (Choice A), when ruptured, typically presents with acute, focal unilateral pelvic pain. If the cyst remains intact, it may be asymptomatic or increase the risk for adnexal torsion if large.</p><p>Dermoid cyst (Choice B) is a common benign ovarian tumor that contains cell types from all three embryologic layers. It may present with symptoms of abdominal fullness and pain or with ovarian torsion.</p><p>Ectopic pregnancy (Choice C) refers to fetal implantation outside of the endometrial cavity, most commonly the fallopian tube, and is often asymptomatic until large or ruptured. Rupture generally presents with acute, severe abdominopelvic pain, vaginal bleeding, and signs of peritoneal inflammation.</p><p>Endometrioma (Choice D) is a cyst that is formed from ectopic endometrial tissue within the adnexa as a complication of endometriosis. Endometriosis commonly presents with dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, and dyschezia. Endometriomas are often adherent to nearby structures and would present as a solitary ovarian cyst as opposed to multiple.</p><p>Follicular cyst (Choice E) is a simple cystic structure of the ovary that occurs during the process of follicle development when an egg is not appropriately released from a follicle during ovulation. In general, there would be one, or few, follicular cysts, not multiloculated cystic ovaries with abdominal distention.</p><p>Granulosa cell tumor (Choice F) is the most common sex cord-stromal tumor and presents with an adnexal mass and evidence of estrogen excess such as breast tenderness and uterine bleeding. Ultrasonography may show a large, complex ovarian mass that may contain septations or cysts. It would not be the most common diagnosis after treatment with gonadotropins.</p><p>Leiomyomata uteri (Choice H), or uterine fibroids, are estrogen-sensitive, benign neoplasms of the uterine myometrium that present with vaginal bleeding, pelvic pain, and miscarriage or infertility. The uterus will be large and irregularly shaped on physical examination. There would not be abnormal loculations or cysts within the adnexa.</p><p>Sertoli-Leydig cell tumor (Choice I) is a sex cord-stromal tumor derived from testosterone-secreting Sertoli-Leydig cells. Patients typically present at younger ages and may develop signs of androgen excess and masculinization.</p><p>Tubo-ovarian abscess (Choice J) presents as a sequela of pelvic inflammatory disease, with fever, mucopurulent cervical discharge, and cervical and/or adnexal tenderness, as well as a fluctuant mass on pelvic examination. Ultrasonography may show the abscess.</p>", "45": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Pregnancy must be considered as a potential cause of irregular menses in all patients of reproductive age. A pregnancy test measuring β-hCG is an inexpensive, rapid, and sensitive test for diagnosing pregnancy and should be obtained before proceeding with further workup.</p><p>Pregnancy should be considered as a cause of irregular menses in all patients of reproductive age, especially in patients with irregular or no use of contraceptives. For patients with advanced maternal age, pregnancy must be excluded as a potential cause by obtaining a pregnancy test before proceeding with further evaluation of other causes such as menopause. Human chorionic gonadotropin is secreted by the placenta in order to maintain the corpus luteum and its production of progesterone. A pregnancy test measuring β-hCG is an inexpensive, rapid, and sensitive test for diagnosing pregnancy and should be obtained before proceeding with further workup. Given this patient's right adnexal tenderness, further workup may include pelvic ultrasonography to rule out ectopic pregnancy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Follicle-stimulating hormone (Choice A) and luteinizing hormone (Choice C) are increased in menopause and premature ovarian insufficiency as a result of the lack of negative feedback from estrogen on the anterior pituitary. This is less likely than pregnancy in this patient of reproductive age with regular menstrual periods and no vasomotor symptoms of menopause (eg, hot flashes, night sweats).</p><p>Progesterone (Choice D) is increased during the luteal phase of menstruation, as it acts to maintain the secretory endometrium, as well as during pregnancy. β-hCG is a more sensitive and specific test for this patient with an unconfirmed pregnancy.</p><p>Thyroid-stimulating hormone (Choice E) concentrations aid in the diagnosis of thyroid disorders. While both hyperthyroidism and hypothyroidism can present with irregular menses, the abrupt change in this patient's menstrual cycle and abdominal pain are more consistent with pregnancy. Additionally, she does not present with any symptoms of hyperthyroidism (eg, heat intolerance, palpitations, weight loss, diarrhea) or hypothyroidism (eg, cold intolerance, bradycardia, weight gain, constipation).</p>", "46": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Cyclic progesterone therapy, or progestin withdrawal therapy, is frequently used to treat anovulatory irregular menses. Progesterone therapy functions to transition the endometrium from proliferative to secretory and inhibits follicle-stimulating hormone and luteinizing hormone secretion to prevent ovulation. When withdrawn, bleeding will occur if the anovulation is related to irregular gonadotropin release but will not occur if there is a structural abnormality of the uterus or estrogen deficiency.</p><p>Cyclic progesterone therapy, or progestin withdrawal therapy, is a key treatment in the management of anovulation. Common causes of anovulation include pregnancy, polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS), abnormalities or immaturity of the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis, menopause or premature ovarian failure, inadequate nutrition, chromosomal abnormalities, and thyroid disorders. Anovulation presents with irregular intervals of menstruation, associated with heavy vaginal bleeding when menses occur, due to continued endometrial development. Progesterone therapy acts to inhibit follicle-stimulating hormone and luteinizing hormone, preventing ovulation, as well as to maintain the endometrial lining and cause its transition to a secretory, rather than proliferative, endometrium. When progesterone is withdrawn, bleeding occurs if the anovulation is caused by an abnormality or irregularity of the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis. These patients have sufficient or excessive/unopposed serum estradiol concentrations. Bleeding will not occur if the abnormal bleeding is caused by a structural abnormality of the uterus or in the setting of estrogen deficiency (eg, premature ovarian failure).</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Gastric bypass (Choice A) is a treatment option for patients with obesity or metabolic disorders (eg, metabolic syndrome, diabetes mellitus) associated with PCOS. This would be premature for this patient who does not yet have a definitive diagnosis. If diagnosed with PCOS, treatment includes combination oral contraceptives, antihyperglycemic medications, and weight loss (which should first be attempted with lifestyle changes).</p><p>Measurement of serum estradiol concentration (Choice B) may detect hormonal changes associated with disorders such as PCOS, pituitary adenoma, or sex cord-stromal tumors. However, estradiol concentrations fluctuate throughout the menstrual cycle and in response to other hormones on the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis. Progestin withdrawal therapy is a more specific and sensitive diagnostic test that also can provide treatment if menstruation is induced.</p><p>Pelvic ultrasonography (Choice C) can be helpful in evaluating endometrial hyperplasia, structural abnormalities of the uterus, or polycystic ovaries. However, administering progestin withdrawal therapy first will narrow down the differential diagnosis and treat the patient's amenorrhea.</p><p>Observation only (Choice E) is inappropriate in this young patient with secondary amenorrhea. Failure to diagnose and treat hormonal and metabolic disorders can lead to numerous complications, including endometrial cancer in the setting of long exposure to unopposed estrogen.</p>", "47": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Perforation of an intra-abdominal viscus may lead to severe, diffuse abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, high fever, tachycardia, and septic shock. Risk factors include peptic ulcer disease, trauma, infection of the bowel (eg, appendicitis, colitis, diverticulitis), invasive malignancy, and iatrogenic perforation (eg, during unrelated surgery), among others. Diagnosis includes clinical suspicion and imaging, which will show free intra-abdominal air.</p><p>Perforation of an intra-abdominal viscus may lead to severe, diffuse abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, high fever, tachycardia, and septic shock. Bowel contents in the peritoneal space cause rapid infection in the form of peritonitis, marked by abdominal rigidity, rebound tenderness, and guarding. Perforated viscus and resultant peritonitis is a surgical emergency requiring exploratory laparotomy. Risk factors include peptic ulcer disease, trauma, infection of the bowel (eg, appendicitis, colitis, diverticulitis), invasive malignancy, and iatrogenic perforation (eg, during unrelated surgery), among others. For this patient, inflammation from endometrioma plus adhesions likely damaged the bowel wall, leading to perforation during exploration and adhesiolysis. Diagnosis includes clinical suspicion and imaging, which will show free intra-abdominal air. Management of bowel perforation with resultant peritonitis includes broad-spectrum antibiotics plus emergent surgical exploration and repair.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</b></p><p>Bacterial stasis and overgrowth (Choice A) results from abnormal growth of bacteria in the small intestine, which disrupts normal digestion of macromolecules. Patients present with recurrent nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, bloating, weight loss, and malabsorption. Bacterial overgrowth classically occurs in patients with a previous history of abdominal surgeries, resection of the ileocecal valve, creation of blind loops, or following prolonged use of proton pump inhibitors.</p><p>Bladder perforation (Choice B) presents with suprapubic and lower quadrant abdominal pain, dysuria, hematuria, and urinary urgency with little urine volume. Imaging would show free fluid within the abdominal cavity without free air.</p><p>Contamination by skin flora (Choice D) is a common occurrence on blood cultures, which are drawn to assess for the presence of bloodstream infection. Methicillin-sensitive Staphylococcus aureus is a common contaminant.</p><p>Opportunistic organisms (Choice E) describes a group of pathogens that infect hosts in immunocompromised states—organisms that would not otherwise be able to infect an immunocompetent person. These include Pneumocystis jirovecii, Mycobacterium avium-intracellulare, and Cryptococcus neoformans, among others. This patient is not immunocompromised in a manner that would permit infection from an opportunistic pathogen (eg, AIDS, late-stage malignancy on chemotherapy).</p>", "48": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Levonorgestrel-based contraceptives induce endometrial atrophy, which prevents implantation and development of an ovum. Patients may experience amenorrhea or irregular menses, with occasional spotting or light bleeding, when using a levonorgestrel IUD.</p><p>An IUD is a long-acting reversible contraceptive option that does not require active compliance on the part of the patient. Both copper and levonorgestrel IUDs are available. Hormonal (levonorgestrel) IUDs cause cervical mucus thickening and induce glandular atrophy of the endometrium, preventing implantation and development of an ovum. Glandular atrophy of the endometrium limits the amount of endometrium that can be shed during menstruation, and many patients using hormonal IUDs will cease to menstruate or will have small amounts of irregular spotting, as in this patient. IUDs are a reliable form of contraception with failure rates as low as 1%. Depending on the type, they may be kept in place for up to 10 years.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, E, and F.</b></p><p>Asherman syndrome (Choice A) describes uterine synechiae, fibrous adhesions that form within the uterine cavity as a sequela of injury to the endometrium. Precipitants include endometrial trauma and infection. The typical presentation includes infertility, spontaneous abortions, and menstrual irregularities, such as dysmenorrhea or amenorrhea. This patient does not have a history of preceding trauma to the endometrium.</p><p>Hypothyroidism (Choice C) frequently presents with fatigue, weight gain, constipation, cold intolerance, and abnormal menstruation. This patient lacks any characteristic symptoms of hypothyroidism.</p><p>Polycystic ovarian syndrome (Choice D) presents with menstrual irregularities, signs of androgen excess, obesity, insulin resistance, and polycystic ovaries on ultrasonography. This patient lacks any characteristic signs and symptoms of polycystic ovarian syndrome.</p><p>Prolactinoma (Choice E) presents with galactorrhea, amenorrhea or oligomenorrhea, plus headache or visual changes depending on the size of the lesion.</p><p>Normal menopause transition (Choice F) may present with irregular menses; however, this patient is 32 years old and has no other vasomotor or genitourinary signs of menopause, such as hot flashes, night sweats, and vaginal atrophy or dryness.</p>", "49": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Androgen insensitivity syndrome (AIS) is caused by mutations in the AR gene, leading to resistance to androgens and resulting in individuals with a 46,XY karyotype presenting with female secondary sex characteristics but no uterus or upper vagina. Individuals with AIS typically present with features such as primary amenorrhea, normal breast development, and absent or sparse pubic and axillary hair, despite having testes and XY chromosomes.</p><p>Androgen insensitivity syndrome (AIS) is a genetic disorder resulting from mutations in the AR gene located on the X chromosome, leading to varying degrees of resistance to androgens in individuals with a 46,XY karyotype, though these patients present with a female phenotype. These mutations impair the function of androgen receptors, which are crucial for the normal development of male genitalia and secondary sex characteristics. Individuals with AIS typically present with features such as primary amenorrhea, normal breast development, and absent or sparse pubic and axillary hair, despite having testes and XY chromosomes. Internally, these patients often have undescended testes but lack a uterus and upper vagina because of the antimüllerian hormone produced by the testes. Diagnosis of AIS involves a combination of clinical findings, hormonal assays showing increased testosterone and luteinizing hormone concentrations, and genetic testing confirming mutations in the AR gene. Management of AIS requires a multidisciplinary approach involving endocrinologists, surgeons, and psychologists. Gonadectomy is often recommended after puberty to mitigate the risk for malignancy, as the risk for gonadal tumors increases substantially with age. Hormone replacement therapy may be necessary to maintain secondary sex characteristics and bone health.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Craniopharyngioma (Choice B) is unlikely in this patient. Patients with craniopharyngioma typically present with headaches, visual disturbances, and symptoms of pituitary hormone deficiencies because of the tumor's location near the optic chiasm and pituitary gland. This patient's visual disturbances were corrected with the use of eyeglasses.</p><p>Turner syndrome (Choice C) is not likely the diagnosis for this patient. Patients with Turner syndrome typically present with short stature, ovarian dysgenesis, and distinctive physical features such as a webbed neck, low-set ears, and a broad chest with widely spaced nipples.</p><p>Gonadal dysgenesis 46,XY (Choice D) presents similarly to AIS; however, these patients lack secondary sex characteristics, such as breast development. Given this patient has breast development that is sexual maturity rating stage 4, this diagnosis is unlikely.</p><p>Vaginal outlet obstruction (Choice E) is unlikely to be the cause of amenorrhea in this patient. Patients with vaginal outlet obstruction typically present with primary amenorrhea and cyclic abdominal pain caused by the accumulation of menstrual blood. Additionally, these patients will develop normal secondary sex characteristics, such as pubic and axillary hair, which are not seen in this patient.</p>", "50": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Hospice care is a multidisciplinary health care model that aims to provide symptom management, emotional and spiritual support, and access to a variety of health care providers. The purpose of hospice care is to alleviate pain and suffering at the end of a patient's life by minimizing hospital admissions as well as diagnostic and invasive procedures. Opioid analgesics are an effective treatment for cancer pain and can be increased to treat progressive pain.</p><p>Hospice care is a model of health care that prioritizes quality of life over prolongation of life, emphasizing the palliation of physical, emotional, and spiritual pain. Measures that prolong life without enhancing quality of life (eg, invasive procedures) are incompatible with the hospice care model. Hospice care is a flexible model that can be delivered at home, in assisted living, nursing care, or hospice facility, at the hospital, or at a combination of these locations. The setting of hospice care depends on the preferences of the patient and their family and may involve different locations for care delivery and death. Based on models of health care reimbursement in the United States, patients eligible for hospice care typically possess a terminal illness from which they are expected to die within 6 months. The decision to pursue hospice care should be made by the patient or others who know the patient well based on the patient's values and/or stated wishes. For this patient, who has decided to seek hospice care, the most appropriate next step in management is to increase her oxycodone dosage. Opioid analgesics such as oxycodone are useful for the management of cancer pain, which can be severe, chronic, and intractable to other treatment modalities. Oral opioids are typically administered on a fixed, scheduled dose. Breakthrough or progressive pain can be treated by increasing the scheduled dose, usually by at least 25%. Patients with uncontrolled pain on high oral doses of short-acting opioids may benefit from transdermal patches or sustained-release formulations.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Additional intravenous chemotherapy (Choice B) is incorrect. This patient presents with advanced ovarian cancer and states that she wishes to discontinue chemotherapy.</p><p>Intraperitoneal chemotherapy (Choice C) is typically used in conjunction with cytoreductive surgery to decrease disease burden in peritoneal carcinomatosis. While this can be used in the palliative setting, its degree of invasiveness is not compatible with the goals of the hospice care model.</p><p>Palliative radiation therapy (Choice D) can be used to alleviate symptoms caused by tumor burden in metastatic cancer. However, the effects of radiation therapy can take days to weeks to manifest, and there is a risk for adverse effects. Increasing the dosage of a medication this patient is already taking is a faster and safer method of alleviating her pain.</p><p>Surgical resection of the tumor (Choice E) is not effective in the treatment of advanced metastatic disease. Moreover, this invasive treatment is not compatible with the goals of hospice care to palliate pain.</p>"};
